window.__BIRINCI_STORIES__ = {"site_title":"Edifying sayings and stories","lang":"en","categories":[{"title":"Faith and spirituality","slug":"iman-ve-meneviyyat","blurb":"Stories of belief, worship, and the inner path.","count":36,"stories":[{"stem":"lawful-morsel","title":"A Lawful Morsel","paragraphs":["Fresh flour and clean water cannot make good dough if the yeast is spoiled. Nor can spoiled dough produce good bread.","To raise a child of good character, one must nourish that child with lawful and honestly earned food.","— Imam al-Ghazali","Moral: A child’s moral formation begins with the food placed on the family table. Lawful earnings and honest nourishment help lay the foundation of sound character.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"calamity-and-blessing","title":"Adversity and Blessing","paragraphs":["If adversity brings you closer to Allah, it is a blessing.","If a blessing draws you away from Allah, it is a hardship.","Moral: A hardship that brings you closer to Allah is a blessing, while a blessing that draws you away from Him becomes a hardship. What matters is where it leads you, not how comfortable it makes you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"church-bell","title":"The Church Bell","paragraphs":["Long ago, a country had a custom: when a commoner died, the church bell rang once; for a nobleman, twice; for a relative of the king, three times; and for the king himself, four times.","One day, a court that people trusted to uphold justice wrongfully convicted a citizen. The church bell rang five times.","The bewildered townspeople gathered at the church. “Father, who could be more important than the king? Why did the bell ring five times?”","“Justice,” the priest replied. “Today, justice has died.”","Moral: When justice dies, something greater than the king dies. Stand by the truth so that injustice does not go unpunished.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"faith-is-half-of-success","title":"Confidence Is Half the Battle","paragraphs":["A school principal invited several of his fellow teachers into his office and said to them:","“You are our most capable teachers,” he told them, “so we’re entrusting you with a group of our brightest students. Over the coming year, give them the best education you can and help them reach their potential. I wish you every success.”","Teachers, students, and parents considered this initiative a very good idea and were eagerly awaiting the end of the school year.","At the end of the year, their test results surpassed those of other students across the city.","The principal congratulated the teachers and said: “I have a confession to make. Those students we entrusted to you were not the most capable children at all - they were average. We chose them by chance.”","Hearing this, the teachers became proud and began to consider the students’ success a result of their professionalism.","“But I have another confession,” the principal continued. “You weren’t selected as our best teachers either. I drew your names at random from a bag. You succeeded because you believed you could.”","Moral: Confidence can bring hidden abilities to life. High expectations may be the first step toward strong results.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dervishs-spoons","title":"The Dervish’s Long Spoons","paragraphs":["One day a dervish was asked:","“What is the difference between people who truly love and those who merely talk about love?”","“Wait, and I’ll show you,” he replied.","He invited several people who spoke often of love and set a meal before them. First came bowls of hot soup, then unusually long spoons. The dervish laid down one rule:","“You must hold your spoons at the very end of the handle.”","They tried again and again, but the spoons were too long to bring to their own mouths. Soup spilled over them, they burned themselves, and at last they left the table hungry and embarrassed.","Then the dervish invited people who understood what true love meant. He served the same soup and gave them the same rule. This time, each person filled a spoon and held it out to someone across the table. They fed one another, laughed together, and rose from the table well fed and grateful.","When the guests had gone, the dervish turned to those who had asked the question:","“Now you see. At the table of life, those who think only of themselves may go hungry. Those who reach out to others are nourished in return. We gain from giving as well as receiving.”","Moral: Those who think only of themselves may remain hungry at life’s table. Those who care for others are nourished in return. Giving is its own reward.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dervishs-robe","title":"The Dervish’s Robe","paragraphs":["One day a wounded bird came before the king and complained that a dervish had broken its wing. The king immediately summoned the dervish.","“This bird says you broke its wing. Why did you do it?”","The dervish tried to defend himself:","“Your Majesty, I wanted to catch it. I approached, but it didn’t fly away. I came closer, and still it stayed. I thought it had surrendered. But when I reached for it, it tried to escape and hurt its wing.”","The king turned to the bird:","“He didn’t approach you without warning. You saw him coming and could have flown away. Why did you stay?”","The bird replied:","“Because he was dressed as a dervish. If he had looked like a hunter, I would have flown away at once. But I thought, ‘A dervish fears Allah. Surely he won’t hurt me.’”","The king was silent for a moment, then ordered:","“The bird is right. Break the dervish’s arm!”","The bird protested immediately:","“Please, Your Majesty, don’t do that.”","“Why not?”","“When his arm heals, he may do the same thing again. Take away his dervish’s robe instead, so that other birds won’t be deceived as I was.”","Moral: The appearance of holiness does not make a person righteous. Judge people by their actions, not their clothing; a robe alone does not make a dervish.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"your-hand-at-work-your-hope-in-god","title":"Work with Your Hands and Trust in Allah","paragraphs":["A dervish spent much of his time in prayer and solitude. He did not want to depend on others, so he also worked each day, buying bread with his earnings and sharing what remained with the poor.","One day in the desert, he saw a fox whose injured legs could barely carry it. “How does it survive?” he wondered. “It cannot hunt. How does Allah provide for it?”","Just then, a lion appeared with a gazelle in its jaws. It ate its fill and walked away. The fox crawled over and fed on what remained.","The dervish marveled at how Allah had provided for the fox. Then he thought, “If the fox receives food without working, why shouldn’t I? I could spend all my time in worship. Surely Allah will provide.”","He stopped working and prayed day and night. But no one brought food, and he grew weak and thin.","Finally, one day when he was exhausted, he heard a voice from the unseen:","“I gave you health and strength. Why choose the fox as your example instead of the lion? Rise and work. Earn your living, and let those who cannot help themselves find support in you. Feed yourself and others.”","Ashamed, the dervish repented and returned to work. Once more, he earned his bread and shared it, abandoning the wish to live at others’ expense.","Moral: A person who looks at the share of the needy despite having the strength is not trusting in Allah - he is simply covering up his laziness with faith.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"drinking-the-sherbet-of-martyrdom","title":"The Sherbet of Martyrdom","paragraphs":["According to this account, mortally wounded soldiers in Ottoman times were given sherbet made with Zamzam water, carried in small barrels, to sweeten their mouths before death. This is said to be the origin of the expression “drinking the sherbet of martyrdom.”","Moral: An expression can carry a history of sacrifice. The words we use preserve memories of the past.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-fasting","title":"Fasting","paragraphs":["During Ramadan, several young men saw an older man eating and approached him with a teasing question.","“Aren’t you fasting, uncle?”","“Of course I am. I’m only eating and drinking.”","They laughed. “What sort of fast is that?”","“I refrain from lies, malice, mockery, waste, insults, gossip, and coveting what belongs to others. Illness prevents me from going without food.” Then he asked:","“And you? Are you fasting?”","They lowered their heads. “No. We’re only going without food.”","Moral: If a person abstains from food and does not abstain from lying, backbiting, insulting, and evil deeds, he is not fully experiencing the spiritual spirit of fasting.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"fortieth-day-rite","title":"The Fortieth Day","paragraphs":["In some Turkic communities, tradition keeps a mother and newborn at home without visitors for forty days after the birth. The custom is said to date back to the Göktürks.","On the fortieth day, a close relative invites the mother and baby to visit and presents symbolic gifts: coins for prosperity; cotton for a long life and a gentle heart, even when the child’s hair turns white; sugar for sweet words and a cheerful face; wheat for abundance at home; salt for a life that never loses its flavor; and eggs for nourishment and strength.","Moral: Customs can hold generations of love and blessings. A baby’s gifts express hopes for an entire lifetime.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"friend-of-god","title":"Friend of Allah","paragraphs":["An elderly man notices a barefoot child, is moved by compassion, and buys him a pair of shoes.","The child holds the shoes in his hands and gazes at them for a moment. Then he looks up and asks:","“Are you Allah?”","The old man’s hands begin to tremble and his eyes fill with tears.","“No, my child. I’m just an ordinary person.”","The child thinks for a moment, then says softly:","“Then you must be a friend of Allah. Last night I cried and told Him I had no shoes.”","Moral: Sometimes kindness does not descend from heaven by itself; it reaches us through good people. Perhaps you can be the answer to someone’s prayer.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"blessings-god-has-given","title":"The Gifts of Allah","paragraphs":["Every gift from Allah is not only a blessing but also a responsibility. Allah will surely ask how you used what was entrusted to you:","If He granted you authority, were you just?","If He gave you high status, did you show compassion?","If He gave you wealth, did you remain honest?","If He gave you a peaceful life, were you grateful?","If He gave you a life partner, were you faithful?","If He gave you children, how did you raise them?","Moral: Every blessing from Allah carries a responsibility; we honor His gifts by using them with justice, compassion, gratitude, and faithfulness.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hajj-pilgrimage","title":"The Hajj Pilgrimage","paragraphs":["During the Hajj, when the time came to cast stones at the devil, one pilgrim began striking himself with the stones instead. A young man nearby asked in surprise:","“Uncle, why are you hitting yourself?”","The man replied:","“My son, why should we concern ourselves only with the devil outside us? We must also confront the devil within, curb our selfish desires, and purify our hearts.”","* * *","The heat belongs to fire, not to the griddle;","true worth lies in the mind, not in a crown.","Whatever you seek, first seek it within yourself —","not only in Jerusalem, Mecca, or the Hajj.","Moral: The deepest struggle is not against an enemy outside us, but against our own uncontrolled desires. True spiritual worth is not found in outward symbols; it begins within.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"forbidden-money","title":"Ill-Gotten Money","paragraphs":["One day, I saw a man drop some money in the street. Usually I would have called out, “You’ve dropped your money!” This time, temptation got the better of me. I picked it up and slipped it into my pocket.","When I got home, I told my wife the story.","“Add a little of our own and let’s go to the movies,” she said. I agreed, and we made plans for the weekend.","“You always promise, then change your mind,” she said. “Let’s buy the tickets online now.” So I did.","I work as a repairman. Soon afterward, a good customer called to offer me a job at his garden on Saturday. I asked whether I could come on Sunday, but he said it had to be Saturday. Of course, that was the day I had booked the movie. I lost a well-paid job.","When Saturday came, my wife and I were about to leave when her parents arrived unexpectedly.","I was ready to say, “Sorry, we’re off to the movies,” but my wife got in first. “We’re going to the movies—come with us!” There was nothing I could do.","My mother-in-law disliked public transportation, so we took a taxi. Naturally, I paid.","At the cinema, I bought two more tickets and popcorn for everyone. After all, the son-in-law had found money in the street; he could afford to treat them! I had spent less on meat for the feast than I spent on that popcorn. They soon finished it, so I bought more. My windfall was already costing me dearly, but the worst was still to come.","After the movie, I ran into an old acquaintance with whom I had a longstanding quarrel. Despite the presence of my in-laws, he insulted me. I lost my temper, and we began fighting. My father-in-law joined in. By the time the police arrived, my nose and his arm were broken. We settled the dispute at the station, but I still had to pay both our medical bills.","Recently, I saw a man buying a doner kebab. When the seller handed him his change, some coins fell to the ground. The man left them there. I couldn’t help myself. “Brother, if you won’t think of yourself, at least think of whoever finds that money! Please pick it up. Don’t bring trouble on someone else!”","Moral: Money taken dishonestly brings no true benefit and may cost far more than it seems to offer. Leave what is not rightfully yours.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"help-yourself-o-exalted-god","title":"Help Them Yourself, O Exalted Allah!","paragraphs":["To the restless, left without a home,","to the orphan, left alone,","to the needy, left hungry and thirsty,","to the sick, left in hardship,","to the wounded heart that waits and watches the road,","to the hopeless who can see no way forward,","to the heedless, left without light,","to the one who still has a wish, who still dreams,","to the faithless, left without You —","help them Yourself, O exalted Allah!","Moral: The last refuge of the helpless, the lonely, and the hopeless is the Creator. Prayer is the language of deepest need.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"if-fate-allows-we-will-meet","title":"If Fate Allows","paragraphs":["Someone who used to say, “We’ll meet soon,” now says, “We’ll meet if fate allows.” I find that strange. My feelings toward him have not changed; I would still be delighted to see him. Yet those words seem to place a barrier between us. I know nothing of his fate, and he knows nothing of mine. Do our two fates know each other? Can they arrange a meeting on our behalf?","Perhaps fate has nothing to do with it. Perhaps this is simply another way of saying, “Out of sight, out of mind.”","I have never liked the phrase “if fate allows.”","Moral: Do not let distance grow between you and those you care about. Instead of leaving a meeting to fate, make time for one another.","Bakhtiyar Sirajov"],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"light-of-the-universe","title":"The Door of the Heart","paragraphs":["A story about the British painter William Holman Hunt describes a picture of a figure standing at a closed door at night, holding a lantern in one hand and knocking with the other.","An art critic asked him, “It’s a beautiful painting, but how can that door open? Have you forgotten to paint a handle?”","Hunt replied with a smile, “This is no ordinary door. It represents the human heart, and it opens only from within. There is no handle outside. Unless someone opens it for you, you cannot enter.”","Moral: The door of the heart opens only from the inside. Love and goodness do not force their way in - you must let them in yourself.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"let-prayer-come-and-find-you","title":"May the Prayer You Need Find You","paragraphs":["Our elders used to say:","Be the hand that helps someone out of poverty, the door a person in need can knock on, the kindness that comforts an orphan, the shoes on a child’s bare feet. Bring relief in hard times, so that a prayer may find you when you need one yourself.","Moral: When you help those in need and show tenderness to the vulnerable, you become worthy of others’ prayers. The kindness you offer today may return to you when you need it most.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"eat-less-and-stay-on-the-right-path","title":"Eating Moderately and Living Honestly","paragraphs":["Moderate eating keeps the doctor away; honest living keeps the judge away.","“Moderate eating keeps the doctor away” means that avoiding excess and practicing self-control can help protect your health.","“Honest living keeps the judge away” means that acting fairly, obeying the law, and respecting others’ rights can help you avoid disputes and legal trouble.","Moral: Eat in moderation and act with integrity, so that you need neither a doctor nor a judge.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"my-alif-has-been-dotted","title":"My Alif Has Been Dotted","paragraphs":["There is an expression in Turkish: “elifim noktalandı.” It comes from writing the letters “alif” (ا) and “ha” (ه) side by side in the old Ottoman script (اه). Because the “ha” after “alif” resembles a dot, people say “alifim noktalandı,” and metaphorically it means “my joy turned to sorrow.” The letter “alif,” with its long, thin appearance, expresses freedom and beauty, while the letter “ha,” with its bent, almost crushed appearance, expresses distress and collapse.","Together, the two letters form the sigh “ah.”","Moral: A single letter—or a single moment—can turn joy into sorrow. Expressions preserve the feelings and stories behind them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"not-leaving-the-right-path","title":"Staying on the Right Path","paragraphs":["Once there was a man who had grown tired of wasting his days in idleness and living off his inheritance. Wanting to lead an honest life and stay on the right path, he went to the king for advice.","After thinking for a moment, the king handed him a jar filled to the brim with olive oil.","“Carry this jar across the city without spilling a drop. If you spill even one, you will pay with your life.”","The king sent two soldiers with him to make sure the order was obeyed. The man concentrated with all his might and reached the far end of the city without spilling a drop. When he returned, he said:","“Your Majesty, I have done exactly as you asked.”","The king asked:","“What did you see on the way?”","That day the city was bustling with a crowded market, but the man replied:","“Nothing, Your Majesty. I was watching the oil so carefully that I never looked away from the jar.”","The king said:","“Remember that. Give the same care to what is right, devote yourself to your duties, and remember that Allah sees you. Then you will not lose your way.”","Moral: A person who gives full attention to what is right and remembers that Allah sees his actions is far less likely to lose his way.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"o-god-heal-our-sorrows","title":"O Allah, Grant Relief from Our Troubles","paragraphs":["By Your names Ar-Rahman and Ar-Rahim, grant relief from our troubles.","By Your names Al-Ghaffar and At-Tawwab, forgive our sins and accept our repentance.","By Your name Ar-Razzaq, bless the sustenance You provide.","By Your name Ash-Shafi, grant healing to our sick.","By Your name Al-Fattah, open for us the doors of goodness.","Moral: When we recognize our helplessness and pray sincerely, the heart finds peace. Prayer is the language of hope and trust in Allah.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"o-god-give-first-to-the-mountains-and-stones","title":"O Allah, Give First to the Mountains and Stones","paragraphs":["An old prayer from the Turkic lands says:","“O Allah, give first to the mountains and stones, the forests, the animals, and the waters. Then give to the people, to my neighbor, and to those in need. If anything remains, give to me last.”","Moral: True generosity means thinking of others before oneself. This prayer carries within it an entire people’s tradition of sharing.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"pray-while-washing-your-dishes","title":"Gratitude at the Kitchen Sink","paragraphs":["As you wash the dishes, offer a prayer of thanks. The dishes show that there was food to prepare, a table to set, and perhaps someone to care for and feed.","Remember those who have neither food to serve nor anyone to share it with.","Moral: Everyday chores can remind us of everyday blessings. Gratitude begins with noticing them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"ramadan-prayer","title":"Ramadan Prayer","paragraphs":["May Almighty Allah accept our prayers, bless our sustenance, heal the sick, forgive our sins, open doors of goodness, and fill our hearts with love and mercy.","May you have a blessed Ramadan!","Moral: Sincere prayer fills the heart with hope and mercy. The wish for blessings, healing, and forgiveness brings a person closer to the Creator.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"sacrificial-meat","title":"Meat for the Feast","paragraphs":["I was in fifth grade. We were managing well enough then. My father, a builder, had joined a housing cooperative, and we hoped to move into our own home within six or seven months.","One morning we woke up to a terrible shaking. There had been an earthquake. Thankfully, nothing bad had happened to us or to those around us. No one in our building was hurt. But although our house and our lives were safe, this event brought another trouble to our family: as a result of the earthquake, construction work had stopped, and my father was unemployed.","No matter how hard we tried to save, the money my father earned from the repair jobs he found from time to time was not enough to support our household.","My father couldn't find work all winter. He would go with hope and return sad.","A few days before Ramadan, he came home beaming: someone had offered him work. “They told me to bring my papers,” he said, hurrying out again. “I need to get them there today.”","A few hours had passed when our neighbor came in with a panicked expression on his face.","“Don’t be afraid, but your father has had a minor accident,” he said.","At the hospital, we found him pale, his arms and legs bandaged. A driver had run a red light and hit him. As we gave thanks that he was alive, he tried to hide his tears. “Why did I survive?” he whispered. “Now I’ve made things even harder for you.”","After a while, my father came home. Our stove wasn’t burning, our house was cold. Several of our neighbors had called the municipality and asked for coal for us. “No way,” they said — we had to register in advance.","Eid al-Fitr came and went without sweets. At least the weather was warmer, and my father’s arm had healed, though his leg was still bandaged.","Then came Eid al-Adha. I thought to myself: “There are twelve apartments in our building, many of them will sacrifice. At least they will give us a share, and my mother will cook my favorite meat dishes.” As I watched from the window, the sacrificial animals were slaughtered, skinned, and bowls of meat were carried to the houses in the empty area opposite. Every time the door was opened, the delicious smell of meat being roasted in the houses spread to our house. But unfortunately, no one brought us a piece of meat.","Meanwhile, my grandmother called from the village to congratulate us on our holiday. She sensed that I was worried by the sound of my voice as she spoke. She asked insistently and anxiously: “Has your father gotten worse?”","“No,” I said slowly. “The neighbors didn’t give us sacrificial meat.”","In the summer months, we would go to the village to my grandmother. She had a cow and five or six chickens. Her cow gave good milk. When she milked it, my grandmother would sing songs to her.","On the third day of the holiday, there was a knock on our door early in the morning. It was my grandmother who came. We welcomed her with joy. She hugged us all, crying. “My lambs, my lambs, I brought you a lot of meat,” she said.","We filled the refrigerator with meat. My sister cut some up to cook, and the smell made my mouth water. Too hungry to wait for the table, I lifted a piece from the pan with a fork. Just as I was about to eat, I heard my father say:","“Mother, why did you slaughter your cow?”","My heart pounded. I dropped the fork and ran outside. The cow she sang to as she milked it, her precious source of income—had she given it up because I wanted meat?","When the table was ready, they coaxed me back. I tried to eat, but the food stuck in my throat and tears filled my eyes. “What’s wrong?” they asked.","“My tooth,” I said. “It hurts.”","Moral: A celebration is complete only when others can share in it. In times of abundance, remember the neighbors who are going without.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"stoning-the-devil","title":"Stoning the Devil","paragraphs":["During the Hajj, when it was time to cast stones at the devil, one pilgrim began striking himself with the stones instead. A young man nearby asked in surprise:","“Uncle, why are you hitting yourself with those stones?”","The man replied:","“My son, why think only of the devil outside us? We must confront the devil within too—curb our selfish desires and purify our hearts.”","Moral: The real struggle is not only against evil outside us, but against the uncontrolled desires within. Purifying oneself is harder than condemning others, but far more valuable.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dervishs-clothes","title":"The Dervish’s Appearance","paragraphs":["A dervish was once asked:","“Why are your clothes so untidy?”","He replied:","“I spend so much time working on my inner self that I have little time left to worry about my appearance.”","Moral: A person who cultivates the inner life does not judge worth by outward appearance. What truly makes a person beautiful is not clothing, but a pure heart and a luminous spirit.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"idle-devil","title":"The Idle Devil","paragraphs":["A dervish lost in thought suddenly noticed the devil sitting beside him. “Why are you sitting there doing nothing?” he asked.","The devil wearily raised his head. “You people cause one another so much suffering that there’s no work left for me.”","Moral: People oppress each other so much that the devil has no work to do. Most evil comes not from outside, but from within us.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"most-beautiful-portion","title":"The Finest Blessing","paragraphs":["The dervish was asked:","“What is the finest blessing a person can receive?”","The dervish said:","“To meet someone who truly wants to listen.”","Moral: Being listened to with care is a form of being loved.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mullah-and-the-scholar","title":"The Mullah and the Scholar","paragraphs":["A visiting scholar came to Tamerlane’s court and asked, through an interpreter, to meet the country’s learned men. None dared debate him. At last, the Mullah volunteered.","At the gathering, the scholar drew a circle on the ground. The Mullah divided it in half, then into quarters, claiming three for himself and offering one to the visitor. The scholar nodded. Next, he held out a fist with the back of his hand downward. The Mullah turned his own hand over, pointing his fingers toward the ground. Again, the scholar nodded.","Finally, the visitor mimed eating. The Mullah took an egg from his pocket and flapped his arms. The scholar laughed, kissed his hand, and the exchange ended.","Afterward, Tamerlane asked the visitor through the interpreter, “What did those gestures mean? We understood none of them.”","“The circle represented the round earth,” the scholar explained. “He drew the equator, then divided the globe into three parts water and one part land. I asked about plant life, and he showed that plants depend on rain and sunlight.","“Finally, I asked about humans and animals. With the egg, he reminded me to include birds.”","After the visitor had gone, Tamerlane summoned the Mullah for his explanation.","“The scholar?” said the Mullah. “I thought he would be a learned man, but he was only hungry. First he drew a circle, meaning a pan of omelet. I divided it in half: ‘You can’t have it all—there’s me to consider.’ He still looked dissatisfied, so I divided it into four and claimed three shares to teach him a lesson about greed. He agreed. Then he raised his hand, asking for a pot of pilaf.","“I turned my hand down to say I’d have the topping. Then he rubbed his stomach to show he was hungry from traveling. I took out my egg and told him, ‘I’m hungrier than you, and this is all I have. Don’t ask me to share it!’”","Moral: The same sign is read differently in each person’s world. We often see the other in the mirror of our own thoughts.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"gods-pleasure","title":"The Pleasure of Allah","paragraphs":["Haja Ubaydullah Ahrar says:","“I have explored every path that leads to Allah’s pleasure. The shortest I have found is bringing joy to others.","“I have explored every path that leads away from Him. The most dangerous I have found is breaking someone’s heart.”","Moral: Bringing joy to others draws us closer to Allah, while breaking someone’s heart leads us away from Him.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"power-of-truth","title":"The Strength of Truth","paragraphs":["Truth’s sword does not bend, and the hand that holds it does not yield.","Moral: Truth is unbending and unyielding. Even though time passes and power changes, in the end, the righteous one stands firm.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"price-of-a-miracle","title":"The Price of a Miracle","paragraphs":["(A True Story)","Lisa was eight years old when she heard her parents talking about her younger brother, Oliver. All the little girl knew was that her brother was very sick and that her parents had no money for his treatment.","Only a very expensive operation could save Oliver. There was no one to lend them money. Lisa heard her father whisper helplessly to her tearful mother, “Only a miracle can save him now.”","The girl went into her room and took out a glass jar that she had kept in a secret place in the closet. She poured out all the money in the jar on the floor and counted it carefully—she did this several times. When she was finished counting, she carefully put the coins in the jar, closed the lid, and went out the back door to the pharmacy.","At the pharmacy, she waited for the pharmacist to notice her. He was deep in conversation and ignored her attempts to attract his attention. Finally, she placed a few coins firmly on the glass counter. He turned, startled.","“What do you need, little girl? I’m talking to my brother. I haven’t seen him in a long time.”","“I need to talk about my brother too,” Lisa said urgently. “He’s ill, and I need a miracle to make him better.”","“A miracle?”","“His name is Oliver. There’s something bad growing in his brain. My parents say only a miracle can save him now. How much does one cost?”","“I’m sorry, but we don’t sell miracles here,” the pharmacist said more gently.","“I have money. If it isn’t enough, I’ll find more. Please just tell me the price.”","A smartly dressed man who had been watching the conversation from the sidelines until now—the pharmacist’s brother—leaned down and asked Lisa:","“What sort of miracle does your brother need?”","“I don’t know. He’s very sick and needs an operation. My parents can’t pay, so I brought my own money.”","“How much do you have?”","“One dollar and eleven cents. That’s all, but I can try to get more.”","“One dollar and eleven cents,” he said with a smile. “What a coincidence. That’s exactly the price of the miracle your brother needs.”","The man took the money from the girl, took her hand, and said:","“Take me home with you. I’d like to meet your brother and your parents. Let’s see what I can do.”","This handsome man was Dr. Carlton Armstrong, a renowned neurosurgeon.","He performed the operation without charge, and Oliver soon came home. His parents were overjoyed. “It’s a miracle,” his mother whispered. “I wonder what that operation would have cost.”","Lisa smiled. She knew the price: one dollar and eleven cents, and a little girl’s faith that her brother could be helped.","Moral: Faith is a powerful force. Sometimes, when a child’s pure faith meets the kindness of a human being, a miracle is born.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"where-does-calamity-come-from","title":"Where Does Misfortune Begin?","paragraphs":["Someone asked a dervish:","“Where does misfortune come from?”","He replied:","“Examine your eyes. Have you looked where you should not?","“Examine your tongue and ears. Have you spread or listened to gossip?","“Examine your hands. Have you taken what is forbidden or earned your living dishonestly?","“Examine your feet. Have they carried you where you should not go?”","Moral: The root of what happens to us can often be found in our own actions. Check your eyes, your tongue, your hands, and your steps.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"why-are-you-waiting-for-the-last-day-of-the-world","title":"Why Wait for the End of the World?","paragraphs":["One day, a professor at a university in America addressed his students as follows while starting a class:","“Imagine the world will end tomorrow. There is nothing you can do to prevent it. How would you spend today?”","The students gave different answers:","“I would pray and ask Allah to forgive me.”","“I would say goodbye to everyone I love.”","“I would spend the day with my family.”","“I would visit my parents.”","“I would eat my favorite foods.”","“I’d sleep as long as I wanted.”","“I would walk through the forest one last time.”","“I would watch the sunrise and sunset.”","“I would look at the stars.”","“I would cook for everyone I love.”","“I would go to the place I’ve always wanted to see.”","“I’d find the people I’ve hurt and ask their forgiveness.”","The professor wrote the answers on the board, then smiled.","“Why wait for the end of the world to do these things?”","Moral: Make time now to love, forgive, and appreciate life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Family and upbringing","slug":"aile-ve-terbiye","blurb":"Parental love, raising children, and building a home.","count":21,"stories":[{"stem":"fathers-footprints","title":"A Father’s Footsteps","paragraphs":["A father said to his son:","“Son, watch your step carefully.”","The boy replied:","“You’d better watch yours, Dad. I’m following in your footsteps.”","Moral: The most powerful form of guidance is personal example. Children often imitate not what adults say, but the steps they take.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"festive-gift","title":"A Festive Gift","paragraphs":["“Wise elder,” I asked, “what should I buy for the holiday?”","He replied:","“Cheer a few people up. Make a few children smile. And, if you can, help someone in need enough to earn their blessing.”","Moral: The most precious gift is measured not by money, but by kindness and attention. What makes a celebration meaningful is sometimes not what you receive, but what you give.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mothers-love","title":"A Mother’s Love","paragraphs":["Erich Fromm","I am one year old—completely happy, because my mother is always beside me.","I am two—my mother is with me only sometimes. I wonder what is happening.","I am three—my mother is at work, and my nanny is with me.","I am five—my mother is in a meeting, and my grandmother is with me.","I am seven—my mother is at a seminar; I am at school with my teacher.","I am thirteen—a teenager. My mother still has to work, and my friends are beside me.","I am seventeen—my mother comes home exhausted. I am at school, in class, or at a café with friends.","I am twenty—young and confident. My mother is not at home, but my friends are with me.","I am twenty-three—I have started working. My mother is home now, while I am at work.","I am thirty—and I wonder: when did my mother and I last spend real time together? Something is wrong.","I am thirty-five—married. My mother lives in her home, and I in mine.","I am forty—my mother waits for me just as I once waited for her as a child.","I am fifty—I am older now, but one wish remains unchanged: to hold my mother close and rest my head in her lap.","I am fifty-five—another mother is always beside me now, the mother of my children. My own mother has passed away, and I live with the ache of missing her.","In the rush of ordinary life, one of our gravest failures is not giving enough time to our children and those we love. Children are the most precious characters in the extraordinary film of life unfolding before us. If we fail to notice them growing, those passing moments can never be recovered.","Life has no pause or rewind button. We cannot replay the moments that have passed. May we understand that while there is still time.","Love your child today. Hold them, take their hand, and remain beside them. Do not miss today’s episode, because tomorrow a new scene of childhood will already have begun.","“A mother’s love is a selfless, protective, and warm refuge. Precisely because it is unconditional, it cannot be obtained by force.”","Moral: A mother’s love is unconditional and irreplaceable. Make time for those you love today, because time is the most precious thing we can never recover.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mothers-advice-fakir-baykurt-never-forgot","title":"The Advice Fakir Baykurt Never Forgot","paragraphs":["As a child, the Turkish writer Fakir Baykurt (1929–1999) once asked his mother for tea. She took him to Topal Hüseyin’s teahouse and ordered a cup for him.","Eager to drink, he took a large mouthful before realizing how hot it was. Startled by the pain, he dropped the cup.","It fell to the ground without breaking. He expected his mother to slap him, but she simply asked for another cup.","This time, he sipped carefully. For years afterward, he asked why she had not punished him. She never answered.","Much later, his mother, Elif Khanum, visited the village school where he taught. He recalled:","“I became angry during the lesson and shouted at the children. Afterward, I asked, ‘Well, Mother, what did you think of my teaching?’","“‘It wasn’t bad, son.’","“‘The inspectors praise me and give me good marks, and all you say is that it wasn’t bad?’","“She answered, ‘For years you’ve asked why I didn’t slap you over the tea. Now listen.","“‘If I had shouted, the lion inside you would have roared back. If I had beaten you, I might have killed that lion. You might never have become a teacher. Be patient, as I was. Don’t use a stick to kill the courage in these children.’”","Moral: Don't kill the \"lion\" inside a child — his self-confidence — by beating him. True education is not given with fear, but with patience and love.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"father-and-son","title":"Father and Son","paragraphs":["After a bitter argument with his father, a young man left home. Hurt and angry, his father forbade anyone to mention his name. Each night, his mother sat on his bed and fell asleep holding his pillow close, breathing in its familiar scent.","“I miss our son,” she pleaded. “Let’s find him and bring him home.” But her husband refused.","A long time passed.","One day, unable to bear seeing his wife in tears, the father took a slip of paper from his pocket and handed it to her.","“You’ll find him at this address,” he said. “But don’t tell him I gave it to you.”","He said a few more things, but she barely heard them. Overjoyed, she hurried out. The address led to a repair shop, where she found her son working in overalls. For a moment she watched him through her tears, then walked toward him. He had heard nothing from his family and believed they had forgotten him. Startled to see her, he pulled her into a tight embrace.","He asked after everyone except his father. Finally, he said:","“How is that man? Is he still angry?”","His mother avoided the question.","“Come home, son,” she said.","“No, Mother. I can’t live under the same roof as him again.” He turned back to his work.","She watched him for a while, then called him over to give him the sweets she had brought.","He accepted them, but said:","“Please don’t insist. I’m not coming home. He hasn’t looked for me once. He doesn’t care about me.”","His mother lowered her head.","“Well, if you’re determined to stay, that’s your choice. But your father asked me to tell you to keep away from the friend you’ve been spending time with this past month. He says that person is no good for you, and that you should make peace with your old friend.”","Her son froze in astonishment.","Back at home, the mother confronted her husband.","“If you knew where he was, why didn’t you tell me? Is that why you seemed so calm?”","The father broke down in tears.","“He’s my own flesh and blood,” he said. “My whole heart.”","“How long have you known?” she asked.","“Since the day he left. Sometimes I went during my lunch break to watch from a distance and make sure he was eating properly. That’s why I came home late some evenings too. I went to see what he was doing and who he was with.”","As the parents held each other and cried, someone knocked. The mother wiped her eyes and opened the door.","Their son stood there. He kissed their hands and embraced them. Now he understood: the father he thought had cast him out of his heart had never stopped watching over him.","Moral: Don't hide your love, show it. Sometimes a timely step can prevent years of resentment.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"from-a-wise-fathers-advice-to-his-son","title":"A Wise Father’s Advice to His Son","paragraphs":["My beloved son,","Cultivate the understanding that helps you build good relationships. It is an inexhaustible source of happiness and joy.","Our desire for life is never fully satisfied. Whether a life is long or short does not determine its worth.","Fruit is ready to fall only when it is ripe. In the same way, people have always valued the wisdom that comes with experience.","In youth, people often do wrong without thinking. Later, the memory of those wrongs can pierce the heart like a dagger.","Those who follow an honest path in youth earn respect in old age.","Strength is a good thing, but it must be guided by reason.","Allah, the Creator of all worlds, did not place us here to live forever. Every life must come to an end.","Through many trials, Allah has taught me two habits that have helped preserve my health: eating modestly and walking after meals.","I also want you to understand that there are three kinds of people.","The first think clearly and can look ahead to the consequences of their actions.","The second lose their way through the influence of others, but are willing to listen when someone offers guidance.","The third know little, yet believe they know everything and refuse all advice. They are the most unfortunate of all.","My son,","If you belong to the first group, I will be glad.","If you belong to the second, listen to good advice.","Do not become one of the third. Such people earn favor neither with Allah nor with others.","Moral: Wisdom is the source of happiness, and strength is the servant of wisdom. He who listens to advice will be exalted; but he who thinks he knows everything will perish.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"grandfather-and-grandson-at-the-market","title":"Grandfather and Grandson at the Market","paragraphs":["A grandfather took his grandson to the supermarket. As they filled their basket, the boy kept shouting, “Buy me this! Buy me that!” Each time, his grandfather said, “Stay calm, Arif. We’ll go home as soon as we’ve finished.” Finally, the boy grabbed a Kinder Surprise egg from a shelf and crushed it underfoot. “Stay calm, Arif,” the grandfather repeated. Then he turned to an employee. “I’m very sorry. I’ll pay for that too.”","After they paid and left, a woman who had watched them approached the grandfather. “I admired how patient you were with Arif,” she said. He smiled. “The boy’s name is Rafiq. I’m Arif.”","Moral: Being able to control yourself in a tense moment is a sign of true strength. Keeping calm sometimes means mastering yourself, not others.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hold-my-hand","title":"Hold My Hand","paragraphs":["As a father and his young daughter crossed a bridge, he grew anxious.","“Hold my hand tightly, sweetheart, so you don’t fall into the river.”","The little girl replies:","“No, Dad. You hold mine.”","Puzzled, he asked:","“What’s the difference?”","“There’s a big difference. If something frightens me, I might let go of your hand. But if you hold mine, I know you won’t let go, whatever happens.”","Moral: Love offers a steady hand. Do not simply wait for those you love to reach for you; reach out and hold on to them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-am-tired-mother","title":"I’m Tired, Mom","paragraphs":["“I’m tired, Mom.”","“Of what, son?”","“Everything. Work, friends, traveling, always trying to please someone. I’m exhausted.”","“Rest your head in my lap, son. Let it all go for a moment. Share your troubles with me.”","“Thank you, Mom. I’m so glad you’re here. With you, I forget my age and all my worries. Please always be here.”","“I’m your mother, son. I’ll always be here for you.”","“We wear you out too, don’t we? Forgive us.”","“You don’t wear me out. I’m tired because I no longer have a mother of my own. There’s no lap where I can rest my head. Treasure what you have, son.”","Moral: A mother’s lap can be our safest refuge. Remember that she, too, may long for someone to comfort her.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mother-and-son","title":"Mother and Son","paragraphs":["The man set the suitcases against the wall, straightened up, and took a deep breath. “It’s a lovely place, Mom.”","“Of course it is, son,” she replied. For a while they stood silently, looking around the room.","Then the woman opened her small suitcase and took out her nightgown, slippers, a few towels, and a framed picture. She placed the picture on the table next to her bed.","In the photograph, a small boy in school uniform stood stiffly, his eyes brimming with tears and his lips pressed together.","The man approached the window and opened the curtain. A pale daylight filled the room. It was freezing outside. He leaned his hands on the heater and watched the muddy cars passing by for a while. Then, without even turning his head, he asked, “Is this closet enough for you, Mom?”","She did not hear him. Seated on the bed, she was gazing at the frightened boy in the worn frame.","It was a picture they had taken on the first day of school.","The memory returned. The day before school began, she had spent hours telling him how wonderful it would be. Even so, she had barely persuaded him to enter the classroom. She waited all day, reassuring him at every break: “I’m here, son. I’m waiting for you.” He clung to her hand. “Don’t leave me, Mom. I’m scared.”","The sun was setting. Her son moved his warm hands from the radiator to the cold window. “Shall we open the other suitcase and put your things away?”","“Don’t let me keep you. I can do it. There isn’t much.” She reached for his hand with trembling fingers. For a moment she seemed about to say more. Then she swallowed. “The children will be wondering where you are.”","The door closed. She was alone with the peeling paint and the photograph. After a while, she put on her slippers and went to the window. Below, her son was getting into his car.","The old woman was afraid. Despite the fact that she had listened for hours the day before to how beautiful this place was, she was still afraid.","His handprint still marked the glass. She pressed her cheek against it, her eyes filling and her lips trembling.","“Don't leave me, son, I'm so scared,” she whispered.","Her words seemed to melt into the misted glass and flow toward her son as he waved from the car.","That night, the other residents heard her crying. Sobs in the corridor were a familiar sound at the nursing home.","Moral: The child who once feared being left may one day leave a parent with the same fear. Caring for parents means attending to their need for love as well as their physical needs.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"only-my-mother-would-weep","title":"Only My Mother’s Tears","paragraphs":["Only my mother’s tears are real; all the rest are pretense.","Moral: A mother’s love is cherished for its sincerity and its willingness to ask nothing in return.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"raising-children","title":"Parenting","paragraphs":["A boy constantly defied his parents, neglected his schoolwork, and refused to do his homework. His mother’s blows and his father’s punishments changed nothing.","One day, on the way to school, his mother saw a crowd around a bear trainer. As the man beat a tambourine, a bear with a ring through its nose performed for the spectators.","“I can’t manage my own child,” she thought, “yet this man can train a bear.”","She approached him. “How do you make that wild animal obey? I can’t get my son to listen.”","“Everything has its method,” he said with a smile. “I have mine.”","“Then please help me.”","The trainer looked at the boy. “Bring him to me for a week. I’ll make him obedient.”","The woman was happy and started taking the boy to the bear trainer every day.","Within days, the boy became quiet and compliant. By the end of the week, his mother was delighted. She thanked the trainer and offered him her blessings.","The neighbors were very surprised by this. That disobedient, rebellious boy was gone, and in his place was a calm, peaceful, well-mannered boy.","His teacher welcomed the change too. Soon, however, a different problem emerged. Though the boy no longer openly rebelled, he stole classmates’ belongings, hid what he was doing, and lied. The teacher summoned his mother. Desperate, she went looking for the trainer, but he had left.","A neighbor suggested that she consult a wise, kindhearted man.","She took her son to him and explained. “Let him visit me for a week,” he said. “I’ll see what I can do.” She agreed.","She began bringing the boy to him.","After two meetings, the man spoke to her with concern. “What has happened to this child? He behaves like a frightened animal.” She explained that she had taken him to a bear trainer because he was so disobedient.","“An animal trainer?” he exclaimed. “You want to help a child develop a conscience, not frighten him into obedience. Beating, punishment, and confinement can produce fear, but fear is not an education.”","“Reach his heart,” he continued. “Be close to him. Hug him, listen to him, and let him know he is loved. When he feels safe with you, trust can begin to grow.”","“Neither the bear trainer nor I can take your place. He needs you, his parents.”","Moral: A child is educated not with fear, but with love. It is not a teacher or an outsider who educates a child—it is a loving parent.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"puppies-for-sale","title":"Puppies for Sale","paragraphs":["A little boy entered the shop after seeing a sign in the window saying “We have puppies for sale.”","“How much do you sell puppies for?” he asked.","“Between thirty and fifty dollars. Each one has a different price,” said the shopkeeper.","The boy emptied his pocket onto the counter and carefully counted the money.","“I only have thirteen dollars and twenty-seven cents,” he said, disappointed. Then he looked up.","“Can I at least see the puppies?”","The shopkeeper smiled and called to his wife. She came from the back room with five plump, fluffy puppies following her. One struggled to keep up. The boy pointed to it.","“What’s wrong with him? Why can’t he walk?” he asked.","“The vet says he was born with a malformed leg bone. Walking will always be difficult for him.”","The boy’s face lit up.","“That’s the puppy I want.”","“You don’t need to buy that one, son. You can have him for free.”","The little boy was very angry. He looked the shopkeeper straight in the eye and said:","“I don’t want him for free. He’s worth just as much as the others. I’ll give you what I have now, then bring five dollars every week until I’ve paid thirty.”","The shopkeeper said:","“But he won’t be able to run and play with you like the others.”","“He still deserves a chance.” The boy bent down and raised his trouser leg, revealing a leg weakened by polio and supported by a metal brace. He looked up and spoke softly.","“I can’t run and play either. We both need a friend who understands.”","Moral: Every living thing needs a friend who will understand and support it. The one who is different is just as valuable as the others; we need to give him a chance.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"rotten-seed","title":"The Seed That Grew","paragraphs":["After his wife died, a man sent his often-ill young son to an orphanage, dismissing him as hopeless.","Years passed.","Years later, he saw his son outside a school. The boy had grown healthy and strong. Filled with regret, the father stared at his bright eyes.","“I never thought you would recover like this. How did it happen?”","With tears in his eyes, the young man pointed to an older woman approaching them.","“When you sent me away, you said a rotten seed could never grow. She adopted me and never stopped caring for me. She told me, ‘Every seed watered with love can grow.’”","Moral: Words either build or destroy. It is possible to elevate or destroy someone with a single word—every seed watered with love will germinate.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"spinach","title":"Spinach","paragraphs":["We lived in a remote village without even a proper road. My mother seemed obsessed with spinach. As a child, I sometimes wondered whether grief over my father’s death had unsettled her.","“You need to eat a lot, it gives strength to the body,” my mother would say, and for breakfast, lunch, and dinner, she would only feed us spinach, with a little cornbread on the side. I hated spinach already, I couldn’t stand to see this vegetable.","“When I’m grown and have a job and a family, there’ll be no spinach in my house,” I told her. She said she looked forward to that day and promised to dance at my wedding, however old she was.","The day came, I moved from our village to the city to study in high school. I somehow managed to make ends meet with the small scholarship I received. Once a month, when I came home, I didn't want to eat a spoonful of the spinach dish my mother put on the table. I only wanted to go back to the dormitory and eat delicious food.","I went on to university, then completed a master’s degree and a doctorate with government loans. By then, my mother was old and bent. When I returned to the village to marry, she kept her promise and danced at my wedding, tears streaming down her face. I did not understand those tears then.","Years passed. My mother passed away two years ago.","One day, I found an old notebook in a chest. In it, she had written: “All I had left were spinach seeds and a little corn to plant. There was no money. I cooked what I could grow so my children would not go hungry. I told them I served spinach three times a day because it was good for them. Saying it broke my heart. This was all I could do, though I loved them with everything I had.”","At fifty, I learned to love spinach. My wife wonders why I buy it so often. She thinks I like the taste. She does not know that its smell brings back my mother’s sacrifices and the sight of her dancing through tears at my wedding.","Moral: We often understand a mother's sacrifice in the face of impossibility years later. Love is sometimes hidden in a plate of simple food.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"fallen-teeth","title":"The King’s Missing Teeth","paragraphs":["A king once dreamed that all his teeth had fallen out. He summoned an interpreter of dreams and asked what the dream meant.","The interpreter said:","“Your Majesty, all your relatives will die before you.”","The king was angered by the answer and sent him away. Then he called another interpreter and asked the same question.","The second man replied:","“Your Majesty, you will outlive everyone else in your family.”","Pleased by the answer, the king rewarded him generously.","Moral: The same truth can be spoken harshly or with tact. Words can wound or heal; wisdom lies in knowing how to speak them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"most-beautiful-gift","title":"The Finest Gifts","paragraphs":["For a father — honor,","For a mother — respect,","For a child — compassion,","For a spouse — love,","For a friend — loyalty,","For society — service,","For the deceased — prayer,","For the world — humanity,","For the hereafter—good deeds in this life.","Moral: Every relationship has its own best gift: honor for a father, respect for a mother, loyalty to a friend, service to humanity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"builder-of-the-nest","title":"The Nest Builder","paragraphs":["The female bird builds the nest.","Moral: A woman’s work builds a home and holds a family together. That work deserves recognition and appreciation.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"value-of-your-family","title":"The Value of Your Family","paragraphs":["One evening, a little boy asked his father, “How much do you earn in an hour?” The father wanted to know why. “I’m just curious,” the boy said. Reluctantly, his father answered, “A hundred dollars.”","The boy looked down, then asked, “Could you lend me fifty?” His father grew angry, assuming he wanted a pointless toy. He refused and sent the boy to bed. The child went quietly.","After pacing for a while, the father calmed down. He had not even asked what the money was for. Feeling guilty, he knocked on his son’s door. “Are you awake?”","He went in and apologized. “I had a difficult day and took it out on you. Here’s the fifty dollars.” The boy brightened and pulled some money from beneath his pillow. As he counted it, his father’s irritation returned. “Why ask for more when you already have money?”","“Because I didn’t have enough. Now I have a hundred dollars.” He held it out. “Can I buy an hour of your time? Could you come home early tomorrow and have lunch with me?”","These words deeply moved the father, and his eyes filled with tears. He hugged his little son and apologized to him.","Moral: The greatest need of children is the attention, love, and time spent with them by their parents.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-am-waiting-for-the-teeth","title":"Waiting for the Teeth","paragraphs":["On a cold winter evening, an elderly couple entered McDonald’s. The husband bought one hamburger, a large portion of fries, and a large Coke. At their table, he cut the burger in half and divided the fries equally. Then he took a sip of Coke and passed it to his wife.","Other customers watched sympathetically. “Poor things. They’re sharing one meal.”","But while the man ate eagerly, his wife left her food untouched, taking only an occasional sip of Coke.","Eventually, a young employee approached.","“Excuse me, sir. Your wife hasn’t touched her food. If she doesn’t like it, could I bring her something else?”","The man smiled.","“Thank you, but we’re quite happy. We’ve been married for sixty years. We share everything.”","Still puzzled, the employee turned to the woman.","“Then why aren’t you eating? What are you waiting for?”","The old woman points to her husband and replies:","“The teeth, dear. I’m waiting for the teeth.”","Moral: No one should be left in need of the one they love. May Allah not leave anyone helpless, even in their own family.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"weeds-must-be-pulled-from-the-root","title":"Pull Out the Roots","paragraphs":["During school holidays, we worked in our family garden. My late father wanted us to learn the value of effort, so he left us chores while he went to work. We watered the melons he had planted. For drinking water, we went to the well in our neighbor Uncle Aghasalim’s garden, since our own well water was unsuitable.","We learned to turn the soil and clear the weeds, but they kept coming back. One day, Father explained, “You must work deeper. Until you remove the roots, the job isn’t finished.”","Moral: Deal with the roots of a problem or a bad habit. Clearing away only what shows above the surface does not stop it from returning.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Love and marriage","slug":"sevgi-ve-evlilik","blurb":"Love, loyalty, and the trials of a shared life.","count":20,"stories":[{"stem":"beloved-is-one-who-gives-love-to-people","title":"Those Who Give Love Receive Love","paragraphs":["Many years ago in China, a young woman named Li-Li married and moved in with her husband and mother-in-law. She soon found it difficult to get along with her mother-in-law. Their personalities were very different, and they argued frequently. These quarrels offended traditional expectations of family harmony and upset everyone around them.","After several months, the constant fighting had made life unbearable for Li-Li and her husband. Desperate to do something, she went to an old friend of her father’s, a spice merchant, and told him her troubles. He prepared an herbal mixture and told her to add a little to her mother-in-law’s food each day for three months. He said the poison would act so gradually that no one would suspect her.","To avoid arousing suspicion, especially her husband’s, he told Li-Li to treat her mother-in-law kindly and cook her favorite dishes. Delighted, Li-Li went home and followed his instructions.","Each day she prepared delicious meals and added a little of the supposed poison. She was careful to be kind, so that no one would suspect her. In time, her mother-in-law’s attitude changed, and she began treating Li-Li like her own daughter. Overcome with remorse, Li-Li returned to the spice merchant and begged him for an antidote. She no longer wanted her mother-in-law to die.","The old man laughed gently. “Dear Li-Li, all I gave you was vitamins. The real poison was in your mind. As you treated her with kindness, that poison gave way to love. Now you have become like mother and daughter.”","Moral: The real poison often lies in our own thoughts. When we treat others with love, hatred can fade and enemies can become friends.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"advice-for-those-who-marry","title":"Advice for Newlyweds","paragraphs":["Wise elders offered the following counsel to a young couple beginning married life:","“Do not compare your marriage with anyone else’s. Do not say, ‘Look at what that person’s husband is like,’ or ‘Look at what that person’s wife is like.’","Do not expect perfection; none of us is flawless. Do not dig into each other’s past—it is called the past for a reason.","Do not expect your spouse to read your mind. No one can. Speak openly about your wishes and concerns, and try to resolve difficulties through conversation.","Accept that alongside your shared life, each of you has a private world. Hedgehogs draw only as close as their spines allow; if they move too near, they hurt one another. Respect each other’s personal boundaries.","In marriage, be neither owner nor servant, but companions on the same road.","Do not promise, ‘Everything will always be wonderful.’ Say instead, ‘When life becomes difficult, I will be beside you.’","Even if you have been upset with each other during the day, remember to say good night before going to sleep. Let tomorrow begin with reconciliation.”","Moral: A happy marriage rests not on comparison or the pursuit of perfection, but on open communication, mutual respect, and care for each other’s personal boundaries.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"do-not-let-your-hearts-part","title":"Do Not Let Your Hearts Drift Apart","paragraphs":["Why do people shout when they argue? If the person is right next to you, why do you need to shout? Can't you talk to him calmly? Why do you shout when you get angry?","When people quarrel, their hearts seem to move farther apart. They raise their voices as though trying to bridge that distance. The angrier they become, the louder they shout.","But what happens when people fall in love?","They speak softly, because their hearts feel close together.","What happens when their love grows stronger?","Their voices drop to a whisper, and love draws them closer still. Eventually even whispers seem unnecessary: a look is enough for them to understand each other.","So when you argue, do not let your hearts drift apart. Avoid words that widen the distance between you. One day, it may become so great that you cannot find your way back.","Moral: Shouting reflects the distance between hearts. Even in an argument, choose words that leave a way back to each other.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"early-marriage","title":"Early Marriage","paragraphs":["I had just graduated from university and begun teaching. One day, I was in the staff room marking exam papers. I was correcting some of the girls’ wrong answers so that they could pass and move up to the next year.","A man came in and asked why I was changing their answers. “If these girls fail,” I explained, “their fathers will take them out of school and marry them off at twelve or thirteen. If they move up, they may be able to stay until they’re fifteen or sixteen. In that time, even the least motivated student is bound to learn something.”","About half an hour later, the principal called me in and asked what I had been doing in the staff room. I told him I had been marking papers.","“Did you meet anyone there? What did you say to him?” he asked.","“Did he complain about me?” I asked.","“Quite the opposite. He told me he had come to inspect the school, but no longer felt he needed to.”","“Why not?” I asked in surprise.","“He said, ‘You have a wise young teacher. Please pass on my thanks.’”","Moral: A teacher’s care can change a girl’s future. Early marriage can rob children of their education; we must not turn a blind eye to it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"friendship-and-love","title":"Friendship and Love","paragraphs":["Write your quarrels on leaves, so that they fall and disappear when autumn comes.","Write your anger on clouds, so that it passes with the rain.","Write your hatred on snow, so that the sun melts it away.","Write friendship and love on the hearts of newborn children, so that as they grow, both spread throughout the world.","Moral: Let quarrels, anger, and hatred pass. Give friendship and love a lasting home in the heart.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-kiss-your-eyes","title":"I Kiss Your Eyes","paragraphs":["Do you know why it is said \"I kiss your eyes\"?","Because the eyes bear so much of our longing, they deserve our tenderest farewell.","Moral: Offer tenderness where longing has left its mark. Love recognizes what another person has endured.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-love-you","title":"I Love You","paragraphs":["They say a nightingale fell in love with a white rose… Every night he would go and perch beside her and declare his love. But the white rose ignored his feelings and paid no heed to his devotion.","One day the nightingale gathered all his courage, perched beside the white rose again, and said he loved her madly. But the rose snapped: “Leave me alone — I don’t love you!” and hurt him. However much he insisted, the rose rejected him.","The love was so strong that the nightingale, shaken by despair, begged the rose’s forgiveness, pulled a thorn from her, drove it into his own heart with all his strength, and dyed the pure white rose red with his blood!","Only then did the rose understand his love. But it was too late. Beneath her red flowers, the nightingale closed his eyes forever.","Since that day, red roses have meant “I LOVE YOU.”","Moral: Affection may go unrecognized until it is too late. Appreciate the love in your life while there is still time.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"loving-from-afar","title":"Loving from Afar","paragraphs":["Two friends were talking.","“I think I’ve fallen for someone.”","“That’s wonderful! Does she know?”","“No.”","“Why don’t you tell her?”","“I would, but I’m afraid of upsetting everything. We’d start going to the movies and the theater, walking hand in hand. Then there might be quarrels, apologies, ‘Why did you look at her?’ and ‘Why were you flirting?’ If we’re going to break up in the end, why go through it all?","“I’m happy loving her from a distance.”","Moral: Fear of losing someone can keep us from expressing our feelings. Yet love left unspoken carries its own loss.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"purple-jacket","title":"The Purple Chepken","paragraphs":["According to an old account from Turkish villages, a bride’s dowry included a purple chepken, a traditional jacket. It lay at the bottom of her chest, with the hope that she would never need to wear it.","If a woman put on the jacket and stood on a roof or in the village square, it was a public appeal for help. It could mean that she had been forced to marry, that her husband mistreated her, or that she wanted to leave him. The villagers were expected to protect her, while her husband faced public shame.","The custom encouraged husbands to settle disagreements respectfully rather than through violence. They knew what it would mean if their wives wore the purple jacket.","A man whose wife made this appeal faced divorce and found it difficult to marry again. Families would not willingly entrust a daughter to someone known to mistreat women. The account credits this custom with helping to deter abuse.","When a woman appeared in the jacket, daily work stopped. Women put aside their milking, spinning, and weaving. Mothers and grandmothers gathered to defend her.","Music and celebrations fell silent. Her distress was treated with the gravity of a death in the community.","Her husband faced such disgrace that he could scarcely leave the house or show his face at the teahouse.","Moral: Violence against women is unacceptable. When society considers it a duty of conscience to protect women, there is no room for oppression.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"rose","title":"The Rose","paragraphs":["If you water a rose too much, it will rot; if you water it too little, it will wither; if you water it right, it will live.","Moral: Love and care need balance. Too much control can harm, just as neglect can; attentive care helps life flourish.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"beware-of-those-you-love","title":"Take Care of Those You Love","paragraphs":["On a sunny day in the park, a woman told the man beside her that the boy in the red shirt on the swings was her son. The man smiled and pointed to the boy in blue: that was his son, Jamil.","After a while, he checked his watch and called:","“Jamil, it is time to go home.”","“Five more minutes, Dad, please!”","The father agreed. Later he called again, but the boy asked for another five minutes. Once more, the man said yes.","The woman remarked admiringly:","“You are a very patient father.”","The man answered with a sorrowful smile:","“This isn’t patience. Last year my older son, Kamal, was hit nearby by a drunk driver. I had never given him enough of my time. Now I would give anything for five more minutes with him. I promised myself I wouldn’t make the same mistake with Jamil.","“He thinks he’s getting five more minutes to play. But I’m the one receiving a gift: five more minutes of happiness watching him.”","Moral: Every extra moment with those you love is a gift. Before it is too late, give them five more minutes today.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-breaks-a-marriage","title":"An Eraser for Marriage","paragraphs":["A father wanted to share a lesson with his newly married son.","The father asked his newly married son for a sheet of paper, a pencil, and an eraser.","“Why?”","“Just bring them.”","The son found paper and a pencil, but no eraser.","“Then go and buy one.”","Puzzled, the son bought an eraser and sat down beside him.","“Write something.”","“What should I write?”","“Anything you like.”","The son wrote a sentence.","“Now erase it.”","He erased it.","“Write another sentence.”","“Father, please tell me what this is about.”","“Write one more.”","The son wrote again, and his father asked him to erase it.","After repeating this several times, the father asked:","“Is the paper still white?”","“Yes. But what does that mean?”","His father rested a hand on his shoulder.","“A marriage needs an eraser too, son. You will both make mistakes and do things the other dislikes. Each of you must be willing to erase some of those marks. Otherwise, the page you share will soon be covered with them.","“If you want love and respect, offer them to each other. And remember the eraser.”","Moral: Marriage is the art of forgiveness. For love and respect to continue, both parties must be able to \"erase\" each other's flaws.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mother-of-pearl-flower","title":"The Mother-of-Pearl Flower","paragraphs":["An elderly couple, both in their eighties, stood before a divorce court after fifty years of marriage. The woman’s eyes were swollen from crying. The judge gently asked:","“Why do you want a divorce?”","After taking a deep breath, the old woman began to speak in a choked voice:","“He has worn me down,” she said, her voice breaking. “For fifty years, he has made me weary of life.”","The courtroom fell silent, interrupted only by a photographer’s flash. Reporters waited to hear what she would say. “I have a mother-of-pearl plant that I love dearly. I grew it from a leaf taken from flowers he gave me fifty years ago. We never had children, so I cared for it like a child. One day, it began to wither.","“I promised to get up before dawn every night to water it. In fifty years, he never once offered to do it for me. Last night, I was so exhausted that I couldn’t wake up. I’ve given this man my life, my hopes, everything, and received nothing in return. I would be better off alone.”","The judge turned to the elder:","“Would you like to say anything?”","Leaning on his cane, the husband began:","“I was a gardener during my military service. That was where I met Fatima. I used to bring her the finest flowers I could grow. Soon after we married, she developed neck pain. A doctor told us that sleeping too long without moving made it worse. He advised her to get up and walk around during the night, but she wouldn’t listen to either of us. Then the plant began to wither. I told her it would recover if she got up at night to water it. She promised she would.","“Every night, I woke her and watched her tend the plant she loved like a child. Seeing her care for it, I felt as though I were that flower.”","He paused, then continued:","“After she went back to bed, I got up to empty the excess water from the pot. The plant didn’t need all that water, Your Honor. Last night, I failed to wake too. We’re getting old. Missing its water wouldn’t harm the plant, but I worried about my wife. She blamed me for not waking her. I didn’t argue.”","At that moment everyone in the hall, including the journalists, was in tears…","Moral: Love is sometimes expressed through years of quiet care. Recognize that care, and remember to speak gently to those you love.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"red-dress","title":"The Red Dress","paragraphs":["The red dress was hanging in my mother's wardrobe. It seemed to shine next to the worn, faded clothes she had worn all her life.","When I came to my mother’s bedside, I could see that she was nearing the end of her life.","Seeing the red dress, I said to her:","“What a beautiful dress, Mom. I’ve never seen you wear it.”","“I never have,” she said softly. “Sit beside me, my dear. There’s something I want to tell you before I go.”","I sat down on the edge of the bed. My mother took a deep breath. “I don’t have much time left,” she said and continued:","“I thought I had always set you a good example. Now I’m afraid I taught you something very wrong.”","“What do you mean, Mom?”","“I believed a good woman should always put everyone else first. Their wishes mattered; mine could wait. I kept thinking my turn would come, but it never did. My whole life became a sacrifice for your father and all of you children.”","“But you did everything for us.”","“Yes, and I forgot to want anything for myself. Your father is sitting in the next room, upset and staring at the walls. When the doctor told him I was dying, he came in and said:","“‘You can’t die. What am I supposed to do without you?’","“I worked seven days a week, the first up and the last to bed. I ate the burned toast and the smallest portions. Every spare coin went on your clothes and books, even things you didn’t really need. I never bought myself anything lovely until last year, when I used the money saved for a washing-machine repair to buy that red dress. Your father was furious.","“‘Where do you think you’ll wear that? To the opera?’ he asked.","“And there it stayed. The only time I ever wore it was in the shop.","“I used to think that denying myself everything here would bring happiness in the next world. Now I believe Allah wants us to enjoy life here too. If I could get out of this bed, I would live differently.”","These were my mother’s last words to me:","“Promise me you won’t do what I did.”","I promised my mother and she breathed her last.","Moral: Loving others should not mean abandoning your own life. Leave room for your needs and hopes too.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"true-love","title":"True Love","paragraphs":["There was an island where all the feelings lived.","One day news spread that the island was sinking. The feelings began to prepare in haste to leave.","Love was in no hurry to leave. She waited until the last moment, as if sure a miracle would save her. When the island was almost gone, Love finally decided to ask for help.","At that moment Wealth was sailing away in a very large boat.","Love asked: “Wealth, can you take me with you?” Wealth said: “No, I can’t. My boat is full of gold and silver — there is no room for you.”","Love then turned to Arrogance, sailing in a beautiful sailboat, and asked for help: “Arrogance, please help me.”","Arrogance replied, “You’re soaking wet. You’ll spoil my beautiful boat.”","Sadness was nearby. Love asked him too: “Sadness, may I come with you?”","Sadness said: “Oh, Love, I am so sad that I need to be alone.”","Happiness was not far from Love, yet so excited that she did not hear Love’s call.","Suddenly a voice said: “Love, come with me.” Love felt so lucky and happy that it did not even occur to her to ask who was reaching out. When they reached the shore, the one who had helped Love left her there and went on.","On the shore, realizing how much she owed, Love told a wise person what had happened and asked: “Who was it that helped me?”","The wise one answered: “It was Time.”","“Time? But why did Time help me?” Love asked.","The wise one smiled: “Because only Time knows how important and precious Love is.”","Moral: Only time knows the worth of true love. Time keeps sincere love alive and lets the false fade into forgetfulness.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-women-have-endured-in-this-world","title":"What Women Have Endured","paragraphs":["What have women endured in this world?","Even in fairy tales, they made her either a witch or a stepmother...","Stories burdened women with every accusation.","A woman was rarely allowed to be herself, though she gave life, nurtured it, and loved without conditions.","Woman...","They told her that her hair was long and her mind was short.","“Your hands are covered in flour. Stay in the kitchen. Don’t interfere.”","Woman, how much you have endured.","Few people knew your worth.","Few people could see your love, your heart, your goodness.","They called you wicked when you asked for your rights and virtuous when you kept silent.","Some mistook your love for a wish to control them.","Sometimes, those you raised to the heavens with your love preferred to stay there and forgot about you.","They did not ask how you were or whether you needed help.","They scarcely noticed whether you were there.","They forgot that you had a soul.","They forgot that you were human.","They wore you down, crushed your spirit, and ignored you.","Yet you wanted only to help, to care, and to make life better.","Your soul wandered in this world in need of love.","They talked about heaven and made you experience hell.","They looked into your eyes and denied the pain they saw. They would not hear the cry within you.","You loved being a mother, looking into your child’s eyes. In that child’s embrace, you found an affection you could trust.","Moral: There can be no peace or love where a woman is not valued. It is the duty of society to see her work and heart.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-is-loyalty","title":"What Is Loyalty?","paragraphs":["It is not forgetting,","Not turning your back on a difficult day,","Not closing the doors...","Not laughing when your friend is crying,","Loyalty is sharing every pain with him...","It is not just a word...","Loyalty means being with your friend,","Balm for the wounded,","Water for the thirsty,","Above all, loyalty is understanding: not discarding a rose because it has thorns.","Moral: Loyalty means staying present in hard times. It is shown through actions, not declarations.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"can-a-broken-heart-love-again","title":"Can a Broken Heart Love Again?","paragraphs":["A man asked a dervish:","“Can a broken heart love again?”","“Yes, it can,” the dervish replied.","“But have you ever drunk water from a broken vessel?” the man asked.","“Tell me,” said the dervish, “have you ever given up drinking water because a vessel broke?”","Moral: Even if the heart is broken, it does not stop loving. Just as one does not stop drinking water because a vessel is broken, so too does a person continue to love.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"woman-and-the-mirror","title":"The Woman and the Mirror","paragraphs":["A woman wakes up in the morning and looks in the mirror. She sees that she has three strands of hair left on her head.","“I’ll braid my hair today,” she decided. She did, and had a lovely day.","The next day she wakes up and looks in the mirror again... She sees that she has two strands of hair left on her head.","“I’ll part it down the middle.” She did, and had another lovely day.","The next day she wakes up and sees that she has only one strand of hair left on her head.","“A ponytail today!” she said cheerfully, and enjoyed her day again.","Another day she looks in the mirror and sees that she has no hair left on her head.","“Wonderful!” she exclaimed. “Today I won’t have to do my hair at all.”","Moral: We cannot control every change, but our outlook can help us meet it with good humor.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"yellow-and-red-flowers","title":"Yellow and Red Flowers","paragraphs":["In earlier times, yellow flowers placed in a house window meant, “Someone in this home is ill; please do not make noise as you pass.” Red flowers conveyed a different message: “A young woman who has come of age lives here; please mind your words.”","Moral: A small, thoughtful signal can express care and invite respect. Consideration does not need a raised voice.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Friendship and human relations","slug":"dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri","blurb":"Choosing friends, compassion, and how we treat people.","count":29,"stories":[{"stem":"compliment","title":"A Compliment","paragraphs":["Maria lived with her mother in a small apartment in New York and worked as a secretary for a large company. Her life was quiet, ordinary, and rather dull. At work, hardly anyone noticed her.","One morning, on her way to the office, Maria saw a newly opened hat shop and went inside out of curiosity. A woman and her young daughter were there, along with another customer looking for a hat.","Maria tried on several hats. One appealed to her more than all the others. The moment she put it on, the little girl exclaimed with delight:","“Mom, look how beautiful that woman looks in the hat!”","The girl’s mother turned to Maria and said:","“That hat really suits you.”","The other customer joined them:","“It really does. You look wonderful in it!”","Maria stepped in front of the mirror. She had never been so pleased with her reflection. Smiling, she went to the counter and paid for the hat.","When Maria left the shop, an entirely new world seemed to open before her. The flowers looked brighter than ever, and the fresh air smelled especially sweet. Even the sounds of traffic and passing voices seemed to form a harmonious melody. Maria felt beautiful and happy.","At the office entrance, the security guard opened the door and wished her good morning. In the elevator, someone asked which floor she needed and pressed the button for her. Throughout the day, her colleagues admired her and told her how lovely she looked, as though they were seeing her for the first time. Her manager even invited her to lunch to ask for her opinion on a new project.","When the magical workday ended, Maria was still smiling. She thought about how suddenly her life had changed and was certain that she owed it all to her new hat.","Maria took a taxi home. Her mother opened the door, stared at her in amazement, and said:","“Maria, you look wonderful! Your eyes are shining just as they did when you were a child!”","“Yes, Mom, it is all because of my new hat. I have had an amazing day!”","“What hat?” her mother asked, puzzled.","Alarmed, Maria touched her head. The hat was gone. She wondered whether she had left it in the taxi, at lunch, or in the office. Then she replayed the scene in the shop: putting on the hat, hearing the compliments, walking to the counter, taking out her purse, and setting the hat down beside the register…","Suddenly she understood. She had forgotten the hat in the shop and walked out without it, her eyes still shining with joy.","The hat had not made Maria happy. It was the confidence and delight awakened by other people’s kind words. The world had not changed; Maria’s way of seeing it had.","Moral: A single kind word can change how a person sees themselves—and sometimes how they see the entire world. Happiness is not found in outward possessions, but within us and in the sincere attention we offer one another.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"glass-of-milk","title":"A Glass of Milk","paragraphs":["A poor boy who sold small goods from door to door to pay his tuition discovered one day that he had no money left and was desperately hungry. He decided to knock on the next door and ask for something to eat.","A young woman answered. Embarrassed, the boy could not bring himself to ask for food and requested only a glass of water. Seeing that he was hungry, the woman brought him a large glass of milk instead.","After drinking it slowly, the boy asked:","“How much do I owe you?”","“Nothing,” she replied. “My mother taught us to be kind without expecting anything in return.”","The boy could say only:","“Thank you from the bottom of my heart.”","As he walked away, he felt stronger not only physically, but also in spirit. His faith in God and in other people had been renewed.","Years passed. One day, the same woman became seriously ill. Unable to determine the cause of her illness, the local doctors sent her to specialists in a large city.","Dr. Howard Kelly was called in for a consultation. When he heard which town the patient came from, he immediately went to her room. The moment he saw her, he recognized the woman who had given him a glass of milk years before and rekindled hope in his heart.","Dr. Kelly personally took charge of her treatment and resolved to do everything he could to save her. After a long struggle, the illness was overcome and the woman recovered.","When the time came for her discharge, Dr. Kelly requested the final bill. He wrote a few words along its edge and had it delivered to the patient’s room.","The woman opened it anxiously. She feared that she would never be able to pay the amount and would spend the rest of her life repaying the debt. Then she noticed a sentence written in the margin:","“Paid in full with one glass of milk.”","Signed: Dr. Howard Kelly.","Moral: A selfless act of kindness is never lost. It is entered into an unseen account and may return to us one day—often when we least expect it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"handful-of-roasted-chickpeas","title":"A Handful of Roasted Chickpeas","paragraphs":["A boy entered a nut shop with his mother and kept insisting, “I want some roasted chickpeas.” The shopkeeper saw that the mother did not intend to buy any and felt sorry for the child:","“Come here, son. Take a handful for yourself.”","“I’d rather you gave them to me,” the boy replied.","The shopkeeper handed him a generous handful and asked:","“Why wouldn’t you take a handful yourself?”","The boy smiled:","“Because your hand is bigger than mine.”","“What a clever child you are!” the shopkeeper said with a laugh.","Moral: Sometimes who you ask matters more than what you ask for. When the giver’s hand is larger, the gift may be larger too.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"pair-of-boots","title":"A Pair of Boots","paragraphs":["One day, a schoolteacher asked her pupils to write a story on any subject and promised a pair of boots to the author of the best one. Every child completed a story and handed it in.","The teacher read each piece and liked them all. Unable to choose the best, she decided to draw the winner by lot.","Each pupil wrote a name on a slip of paper, folded it, and placed it in a box on the teacher’s desk. After mixing the slips, she drew one and announced:","“Amina!”","The classroom erupted in applause. Amina pressed the boots to her chest and could not hold back tears of joy.","That evening, the teacher returned home and tearfully told her husband what had happened. He was puzzled:","“You found a wonderful solution. Why are you crying?”","The teacher replied:","“Because every slip said Amina. Not one child wrote their own name. They all wanted the girl from the poorest family to have those boots.”","Moral: The finest victory is to value another person’s joy above one’s own. True compassion is revealed in the willingness to surrender an opportunity for someone else.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-alone","title":"Loneliness","paragraphs":["I visited an acquaintance and found him sitting quietly with his head bowed.","“Are you sad?” I asked.","He looked up. “No. I’m lonely.”","Moral: The biggest pain is to feel lonely even among people. Seeing the loneliness of someone next to you is true sensitivity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"discussion-and-conflict","title":"Discussion and Conflict","paragraphs":["As they did every day, Javid Bey and his team met to discuss their project. Each person briefly described the previous day’s progress and any difficulties they had encountered.","It was Kamal’s turn. Like the others, he reported on the work he had done, but — as if hoping for help from his colleagues — he spoke in more detail about the problem he faced. As soon as he finished, Nadir accused him in front of everyone: “You are to blame; because of you our work has stopped and we can do nothing.” The room froze. Kamal was deeply embarrassed and did not know what to do.","Before the exchange could escalate, Javid Bey, the project manager, stepped in. “Let’s stop here for today and return to this later,” he said. The team dispersed.","Javid Bey decided to speak to Nadir privately. He invited him to the company café for tea. After a little small talk, he said calmly, “My son, the way you treated Kamal today was wrong. You accused him without understanding the situation, and you did it in front of everyone. Put yourself in his place. He came to us for help and received blame instead. I’m sure he hoped we would think the problem through together and find a solution.”","Nadir lowered his head in shame. Javid Bey’s counsel had worked; it was a good lesson for Nadir — he understood he had done wrong.","Later, Nadir would recall the conversation with gratitude whenever the subject arose. No one else at the company ever learned what Javid Bey had said to him.","Moral: Conflict asks who is right; discussion asks what is right. If you want the truth, discuss — do not quarrel.","Bəxtiyar Siracov"],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"everyone-is-the-same-here","title":"Everyone Is Equal Here","paragraphs":["A young, newly appointed municipal leader decided to explore his district on foot. After walking around alone, he stopped for tea at a teahouse on a side street.","“Uncle, may I polish your shoes?” asked a boy of twelve or thirteen.","His shoes were already polished, but he did not want to disappoint the boy.","“All right. If you do a good job, I’ll pay you double.”","“I do the same good job for everyone,” the boy replied.","The official said:","“What do you mean?”","The child answered:","“Our teacher told us to treat everyone equally, whatever we do. I’m earning money to buy medicine for my mother. If I treat you differently, that medicine won’t make her better!”","Deeply moved, the official gave the boy the largest banknote in his pocket and his business card. He promised to help the fatherless child, who was attending school while caring for his sick mother.","He also visited the teacher to thank him for teaching the boy such honesty.","To keep the lesson before him, he had the words “Everyone is equal here” written on the back of his desk nameplate.","Though it was hard, he always tried to put it into practice.","Moral: Treat a person the same because they are human, not because of their rank. Society is changed by those who choose what is right in small moments.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-forgive","title":"Forgiveness","paragraphs":["Forgiving someone does not mean you must love them, speak to them, or continue the relationship. Nor does it mean declaring them innocent or right.","It means beginning to free yourself from resentment and hatred, so that the wound no longer governs your life.","Forgive and be free. Forgive, but never forget, otherwise you will be hurt again.","Moral: Forgiving is not about justifying the other person - it is about freeing yourself from the prison of hatred and resentment. Forgive and be relieved, but do not forget the lesson.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-meet-and-to-know","title":"Meeting and Knowing","paragraphs":["For some, we can only say this:","We were happy to meet them; we were sad to discover who they were.","Moral: First impressions do not always survive familiarity. Knowing someone better can bring disappointment.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"go-but-come-again","title":"Come Again Soon","paragraphs":["In an Ottoman custom, a host placed a departing guest’s shoes at the door with their toes pointing into the house. Putting them on turned the guest back toward the home, as though returning. The gesture meant, “Go safely, and come again.”","Moral: A subtle gesture can convey great kindness. The value given to a person is often seen in small moments.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"gossip","title":"Gossip","paragraphs":["A rude, quarrelsome man was brought before the village elder for spreading gossip.","“It was only a joke!” he protested. “People misunderstood me. I did nothing wrong.”","But the victim of the gossip stood firm:","“You’ve ruined my good name,” said the person he had maligned. “I want justice.”","Under pressure, the man backed down.","“I take it all back. Please forgive me.”","The elder listened, thinking that the man still did not understand the harm he had done and might repeat it. Then he said:","“Take a feather pillow to the market square, cut it open, and scatter the feathers. Then gather every one and bring them to me. Only then can you be forgiven.”","Reluctantly, the man obeyed. “The old man has lost his mind,” he thought. But the moment he cut the pillow, the wind carried the feathers in every direction. He chased them back and forth, yet managed to recover only a handful.","He lowered his head and returned to the elder:","“I couldn’t gather the rest,” he admitted.","Then he sighed deeply:","“Just as I can’t take back the words I spread.”","Moral: A word that is spread is like a feather thrown to the wind - it cannot be collected again. Therefore, stay away from gossip.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"how-to-ward-off-insults","title":"Refusing an Insult","paragraphs":["(An instructive story from the life of the samurai)","A wise samurai taught his students both wisdom and martial arts. One day, a warrior known for cruelty and rudeness interrupted a lesson. He usually defeated opponents by insulting them until they grew angry and made mistakes. He tried the same tactic on the samurai, who ignored him and continued teaching.","After several unsuccessful attempts to provoke him, the warrior left in a rage.","The students, who were watching this scene carefully, asked their teacher:","“Master, why did you let him insult you? Why didn’t you fight?”","The samurai replied:","“If someone offers you a gift and you refuse it, who owns it?”","“The person who offered it,” the students answered.","“The same is true of envy, hatred, and insults. If you refuse to accept them, they remain with the person who brought them.”","Moral: You need not take another person’s malice into your heart. Meet an insult with composure rather than more hostility.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"humanity","title":"Humanity","paragraphs":["Kamal had studied hard for years. At last, the day of the exam that would shape his future arrived. His father, Kazim, was demanding and followed his progress closely. He was certain his son would succeed.","Kamal found his exam room. Nervous at first, he relaxed when he read the questions. He answered them easily and felt sure he had done well. With half an hour left, he glanced toward the window.","Something outside caught his attention. Without hesitation, he jumped up and ran for the door.","“Come back!” the examiner called. “If you leave, your exam will be invalid. You’ll lose a whole year!” But Kamal kept running and left through the back door.","When Kazim came home a few hours later, he scolded his son and slapped him. He demanded to know why Kamal had abandoned the exam. Kamal stayed silent, certain that an explanation would only make his father angrier.","For the rest of the year, Kazim repeatedly reproached him. Hurt but quiet, Kamal kept his head down and continued studying.","The next year, he passed and entered medical school. He became an excellent doctor. Although he had lived in the city for years, he volunteered to work at a remote village hospital. A year later, his family came to visit. By then, the quarrel over the exam seemed long forgotten.","One snowy evening, the telephone rang. Someone in a nearby village needed a doctor. Kamal took his bag, and Kazim insisted on accompanying him along the mountain roads. After an hour through the blizzard, they reached the house. An older man opened the door and led them to his sick wife.","Kamal examined her and arranged treatment. She thanked him in a trembling voice. “You came all this way in the cold, my son. What would the world be without people like you? Being truly human isn’t easy. Let me tell you something.","“Years ago, I was in the city when I was suddenly overcome by severe pain. The streets were empty—there was an exam that day. A young man rushed over and helped me to the hospital. It took half an hour in the heat. I was pregnant and terrified for my child. The doctor said that ten minutes later might have been too late for me. My son was born that day.","“The young man told me his name was Kamal. ‘I saw you through the exam-room window,’ he said. ‘You were alone, so I came to help. I’ve lost a year, but I don’t regret it.’","“I named my son Kamal and raised him on that story. Now he is a teacher, and he tries above all to teach his students humanity. I wish I could find that young man and tell him what I owe him. Anyone can become a doctor, a teacher, or an engineer. The hardest thing is to be a good human being. He did not leave me alone in the road.” Dr. Kamal answered softly, “You owe me nothing, Mother. Be at peace.”","Kazim tried to hide his tears. But once they left, they streamed down his face. “Kamal, my son,” he began, and could say no more.","Moral: A profession tells us what someone does; kindness shows who they are. One act of compassion can inspire generations.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-want-a-friend","title":"I Want a Friend","paragraphs":["I want neither wealth nor possessions,","I want a true friend.","What possession will mourn its owner after death?","I want a friend who will remember me with love.","Moral: A loyal friend is a greater treasure than any possession. Friendship can keep our memory alive after we are gone.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"if-the-road-does-not-tire-you-it-is-because-of-your-companion","title":"A Good Companion Lightens the Journey","paragraphs":["My mother used to tell me, “I was very young when I married your father. I could barely cook or sew. One day, before visiting a neighbor, your grandmother laid a freshly washed blanket in front of me. ‘Finish stitching this before I get back,’ she said. I tried, but the needle would hardly pass through the thick fabric. I kept pricking my fingers. Soon I was crying from the pain and from fear of what I would tell her.","“Then your father came home for lunch. Seeing my tears, he took the needle, quickly finished the stitching, and put the blanket aside. He ate and returned to work without mentioning it to his mother. When she came home, she was delighted to find the work done.”","As the saying goes, “When the road does not tire you, thank your companion.”","Moral: It's not the road that tires you or lightens your load, but the person next to you. A true companion makes even the hardest task easier.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"meddling-in-others-lives","title":"Meddling in Other People’s Lives","paragraphs":["May Allah never leave anyone so idle that they have time to meddle in other people’s lives!","Moral: Unwanted advice can become a burden. Calling interference “kindness” does not prevent it from doing harm.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"know-your-friend","title":"Know Your Friend","paragraphs":["To know a friend’s character, watch how they face trouble, hardship, and disagreement. Then see how they act when their own interests are at stake.","Moral: A friend is known in trouble, in hardship, and in the test of advantage. Everyone is a friend in good times; a true friend is revealed in adversity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"compassion","title":"Compassion","paragraphs":["It was a cold winter night, and I was walking home from work. Ahead of me, a man was swaying and clinging weakly to a lamppost. He looked drunk. As I approached, I saw that he was in his thirties, clean and neatly dressed. Some passersby made loud remarks about the dangers of alcohol; others laughed at him.","“Are you all right? Do you need help?” I asked.","His answer came out as a groan.","“I’m ill.”","I put an arm around his waist to steady him and helped him to a taxi stand. The snow, which had stopped earlier that evening, was falling again. The only movement on the empty streets came from stray dogs chasing one another across the freezing roads.","I was beginning to give up hope when a taxi pulled up. I explained what had happened and told the driver we needed to hurry.","We settled the man in the back seat and drove to the hospital. The doctors treated him immediately and gave him medication. The driver and I waited at his bedside.","The doctor on duty told us that, at the very least, we had saved him from freezing. Still unable to speak, the man looked at us and smiled. When he recovered enough to leave, we drove him home.","His wife knew that he was prone to diabetic comas. Terrified, she had gone out to look for him with their son. When they saw us, they rushed over. The family embraced, their eyes shining with joy.","After hours of worry, our tiredness vanished. The family could not find words to thank us, and their happiness became our own.","When we said goodbye, I asked the driver what I owed him.","He shook his head:","“You owe me nothing, my friend. I’m the one who owes you,” he said. “You let me share in something good. I had almost forgotten how it felt to cry—it must have been twenty years. Thank you for reminding me.”","I hugged the driver and set off home on foot, no longer feeling the night’s cold.","Who knew? Perhaps I would meet someone else who needed help along the way.","Moral: Compassion gives humanity its meaning. It means more than sharing someone’s tears; it means looking for a way to ease their suffering.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"people-who-need-something-from-you","title":"People Who Need You","paragraphs":["Instead of resenting people who contact you only when they need something, consider that they may be struggling and trust you enough to ask for help. That thought may bring you peace.","Moral: Instead of resenting those who seek you only when they need something, think that they come because they trust you — and your heart will be at ease.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"permission-or-apology","title":"Permission or Apology","paragraphs":["A saying attributed to Rear Admiral Grace Hopper suggests that it is easier to ask forgiveness than to get permission.","The idea is to take the initiative, then apologize if necessary, rather than allow endless requests for approval to prevent useful action.","Sometimes waiting for permission is a missed opportunity. Do what you know is right, and apologize later if necessary.","Imagine that a company has a clear problem that is slowing down the work process. For example, every week the same information is manually transferred to several different spreadsheets, which takes hours. An employee realizes that this can be solved with simple automation.","An approval request might pass from one manager to another for weeks. Instead, the employee recognizes that a limited test poses little risk, sets up a separate system that leaves the original data intact, and demonstrates the result. If it works, everyone benefits. If the lack of prior approval causes concern, the employee can explain the aim and acknowledge any rule they have broken.","Moral: When an action is low risk, reversible, and clearly useful, excessive approval processes can stifle initiative. Responsible action sometimes achieves more than endless waiting.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"how-we-treat-people","title":"Keeping the Right Distance","paragraphs":["Treat your closeness to others as you would your closeness to a fire:","don't go too far away lest you freeze;","don't come too close lest you burn.","Saadi Shirazi","Moral: Relationships need balance. Stay close enough to share warmth, but leave enough space to avoid being hurt.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mockery-is-unacceptable","title":"Teach Children Not to Mock","paragraphs":["We should teach our children from a young age that it is unacceptable to ridicule or humiliate other people for their differences or appearance.","Moral: Teach children early that differences are no excuse for ridicule. Every person deserves dignity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"spend-your-time-with-people","title":"Spend time with people","paragraphs":["My father needed to transfer money, and we spent an hour at the bank. Growing impatient, I said:","“Dad, why don’t we set up online banking for you?”","“What would that do for me?”","“You wouldn’t have to spend an hour here just to make a transfer. You could shop online too. It would be so much easier.”","I was very excited about the idea of getting my father involved in the world of the internet.","My father asked:","“Then I wouldn’t need to leave the house?”","“That’s right.”","I explained that even groceries could be delivered to his door.","My father's answer left me speechless:","“In this hour, I’ve seen four friends and talked to staff who have known me for years.","“I get lonely, son. I like dressing to go out and seeing people. I have time, and I enjoy talking face to face.","“When I was ill two years ago, the man from the fruit shop came to visit. He sat beside my bed because he cared how I was.","“When your mother fell on her morning walk, our grocer helped her up and drove her home. He knew where we lived.","“Wouldn’t I lose those connections if I did everything online?","“Why should I trade them for a screen just to have things delivered?","“I want to know the person I buy from. I want a human relationship.","“Can Amazon give me that?","“Technology is useful, but it isn’t life.","“Spend time with people, son.”","Moral: Technology provides convenience, but it does not replace life. The real value is in live communication, in the warmth between people.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"friendship-of-horses","title":"The Friendship of Horses","paragraphs":["Two horses live on a farm near my house. From a distance, they look quite ordinary. Up close, you notice something unusual: one is blind. Its owner has provided a safe, comfortable stable and continues to care for it.","As you approach, you hear a bell attached to the smaller horse’s bridle. The blind horse follows the sound.","Watch closely, and you will see that the horse with the bell keeps checking on its companion, while the blind horse listens and follows at its own pace.","Each evening on the way back to the stable, the smaller horse stops and looks around, making sure its friend is close enough to hear the bell.","Moral: The Lord does not abandon us in difficult times. Help often reaches us through other people, and sometimes we are called to be that help.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"sound-of-the-doorbell","title":"The Sound of the Doorbell","paragraphs":["I was laid off last winter. To make ends meet, I had to work as a newspaper carrier temporarily. Since the mailbox of one of the houses I delivered newspapers to was sealed, I had to knock on the door. An old man opened the door.","“Why have you sealed your mailbox?” I asked.","“I did it deliberately.”","Then he smiled strangely and continued:","“Please knock or ring each day and give me the paper in person.”","“Of course, but wouldn’t the mailbox be easier for both of us?”","“It’s no trouble. I’m always here.","“My wife has died, my son lives abroad, and I’m alone. Who knows how much time I have left?”","At that moment, I saw the old man’s misty, moist eyes.","“Do you read the paper?” I asked.","“Never. What I subscribed to was the sound of the doorbell.”","Then he clasped his hands together and said:","“Please do one more thing for me. Here is my son’s number. If I ever fail to answer when you knock, call him.”","Moral: For some people, one ring of the doorbell, one greeting, is worth the whole world. Let's not forget to knock on the door of the lonely.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"those-you-should-not-befriend","title":"Choose Your Friends Wisely","paragraphs":["Be wary of an arrogant friend who has no understanding of your feelings.","Be wary of someone convinced of their own brilliance, who talks endlessly but listens little.","Be wary of a coward who will not stand by you when courage is needed.","Moral: Arrogance, conceit, and cowardice can make friendship painful. Look for kindness, humility, and loyalty.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"welcome-my-bey","title":"Welcome Home","paragraphs":["When my father, Hafiz Ali, went to Çanakkale, my mother was seven months pregnant with me. I never met him. We did not even have a photograph.","Those were very difficult days. Mobilization, occupation, liberation, poverty, hardship...","Our childhood was spent constantly running around in search of bread, filled with pain.","Whenever my mother wanted to go somewhere, she would always come to me and say:","“Son, I’m going to the market. Tell me at once if your father comes.”","“I’m going to see relatives. Tell me at once if your father comes.”","“I’m going next door. Tell me at once if your father comes.”","She was always watching the road, waiting for him.","I grew up and opened a shop. Whenever she went out, she stopped there to tell me where she was going. “Let me know if your father comes,” she always added.","Years passed, and she grew old. Leaning on her cane, she still came to remind me, “Call me if your father returns.”","One day, she became ill and fell into bed.","On her deathbed, she thanked us for caring for her and asked our forgiveness. Then she turned to me. “If your father comes, tell him your mother always waited for him.” Suddenly, she looked toward the door and smiled. “Welcome home, my bey. Welcome.” Then she died.","* * *","From the memoirs of Cevdet Algalp, an old shoemaker living in Balıkesir.","Written by: researcher and writer, geography teacher at Bursa Çınar Anatolian High School Mustafa Doğru.","* * *","March 18 is the day of the Çanakkale Victory.","The account reflects on what defeat at Çanakkale in 1915 might have meant: foreign-backed states in Anatolia, conflict among its peoples, and domination by Britain, France, and Italy.","* * *","May all who fell at Çanakkale, including the Azerbaijani soldiers, rest in peace. May no mother spend her life watching the road for someone who will never return.","Moral: A lifetime of waiting bears witness to love and loss. Remember both those who died for their homeland and the families who waited for them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"why-people-shout-when-they-argue","title":"Why Do We Shout?","paragraphs":["One day during class, a teacher asked his students:","“Why do people shout when they argue?”","“Because they lose their temper,” a student replied.","“But the other person is right beside them. Why raise their voices instead of speaking calmly?”","Each of the students gave an answer, but none of them satisfied the teacher. Finally, he said:","“When people quarrel, their hearts seem to move apart. They shout as though trying to make themselves heard across that distance.”","After a moment of silence, the teacher continued:","“When people love each other, they speak softly. As they grow closer, they may need only a whisper. Sometimes a look is enough. So when you argue, do not use words that drive your hearts farther apart.","“One day, the distance may become too great to find your way back.”","Moral: Screaming is the sound of hearts drifting apart. A whisper is enough in love — don't increase the distance between you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"your-friends","title":"Your True Friends","paragraphs":["A young man preparing for his wedding asked his father for help. “I can’t manage everything. Would you call the friends on this list and invite them?” His father agreed.","On the wedding day, the young man approached him anxiously. “Did you call everyone?”","“Yes, son.”","“There were fifty names. Only fifteen are here.”","“I called all fifty. I told them you were in trouble and needed their help at this place and time. Don’t worry, son. Your true friends are here.”","Moral: A true friend is one who is there for you when you are in trouble. It is not the many acquaintances that are valuable, but the few friends who stand the test.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Morals and character","slug":"exlaq-ve-xarakter","blurb":"Honesty, generosity, and personal integrity.","count":42,"stories":[{"stem":"generous-man","title":"A Generous Man","paragraphs":["A generous man was once asked:","“Do you expect the poor to be grateful to you for everything you share with them?”","He replied:","“I am like a ladle in a cook’s hand. The ladle serves the food, but the gift comes from the cook.”","Moral: Those who do good should not consider themselves the true owners of what they give. They are only instruments; the true Giver is Allah.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mature-person","title":"A Mature Person","paragraphs":["There comes a time when you value old friends’ sincerity more than passing acquaintances. You step away from ignorance and relationships that disturb your peace. That is one sign of growing maturity.","Moral: Maturity is the ability to step away from harmful relationships and protect one’s inner peace. Knowing whom—and what—to keep at a distance is also a form of wisdom.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"meaningless-question","title":"A Meaningless Question","paragraphs":["One day, a graduate of the University of California, Berkeley, visited a rabbi in New York and asked for help studying the Talmud.","“Do you know Aramaic? No? What about Hebrew? Were you at least taught the Torah as a child?”","The young man answered no to every question.","“But I defended a dissertation on Socratic logic! I would like to study the Talmud to fill some gaps in my education.”","“The Talmud is one of the greatest works of human thought, but you are not yet ready to study it. Still, since you insist, I will give you a logic question. If you answer correctly, I will teach you. Two people climb down the same chimney. One emerges with a clean face and the other with a face covered in soot. Which one will go and wash?”","“You call that a logic question?” the Berkeley graduate asked confidently. “The person with the dirty face, of course!”","“Wrong. The person with the clean face will see the other’s sooty face, assume that his own face is dirty too, and go to wash. The person covered in soot will see a clean face in front of him and suppose that he has remained clean.”","“A very clever answer! Could you give me another chance?”","“All right. Two people climb down the same chimney. One has a dirty face and the other a clean one. Which will go and wash?”","“But we have just discussed that. The person with the clean face will go.”","“Wrong again. Both will wash. The person with the clean face will see his companion’s dirty face and think that he is dirty too. The other will notice that his companion looked at him and then went to wash, so he will realize that his own face must also be dirty.”","“I hadn’t thought of that. May I try once more?”","“Very well. I will ask for the last time: two people climb down the same chimney. One has a clean face and the other a dirty one. Which will go and wash?”","“Now it is clear: both of them will go!”","“No. Neither will go. The person with the dirty face will see a clean face before him and assume that he is clean as well. The person with the clean face will notice that his companion is not going to wash and conclude that there is no need to do so.”","Growing desperate, the young man begged the rabbi for one more question. The rabbi began:","“Two people climb down the same chimney. One has a dirty face and the other a clean one…”","“Neither of them will wash!” the young man answered hastily.","“Wrong again,” the rabbi said calmly. “You studied Socratic logic. Ask yourself: how could two people climb down the same chimney together and only one get soot on his face? The question itself makes no sense. Spend your life answering meaningless questions, and neither the answers nor the search will give you meaning.”","Moral: An answer to a meaningless question cannot lead to truth. A well-formed question is already half the answer.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"attitude-toward-a-problem","title":"How to Face a Problem","paragraphs":["Is there a problem in your life?","If not, there is no reason to worry.","If there is, can you solve it?","If you can, there is no reason to worry.","If you cannot, there is still no reason to worry.","Moral: If a problem can be solved, work toward a solution. If it cannot, worrying will not change it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"avoid-these-questions","title":"Avoid These Questions","paragraphs":["Do not ask someone who is unemployed, “Are you still out of work?” Avoid asking an employee, “How much do you earn?” someone without children, “Don’t you have children yet?” or a single person, “When are you getting married?”","Nor should we embarrass someone by asking, “You didn’t know that?” What seems like ordinary curiosity to us may touch another person’s deepest wound.","Moral: Some questions wound the heart. Every painful question left unasked may lift a burden from someone’s shoulders.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"be-sure-of-your-purpose","title":"Be Sure of Your Purpose","paragraphs":["Two men were struggling to move a heavy chest through a doorway. After a long effort, they stopped, exhausted and drenched in sweat.","One said:","“What a heavy chest! We still haven’t managed to get it inside.”","The other asked in surprise:","“Weren’t we supposed to take it outside?”","Moral: Clarify the goal before beginning the work. Otherwise, you may labor for hours while directing all your strength the wrong way.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-cool-headed","title":"Keeping a Cool Head","paragraphs":["Assess a difficult situation calmly. A clear head gives you a better chance of making a sound decision and finding a way forward.","Moral: The best decision is made with a calm mind, not in a panic. Cool-headedness can turn even a difficult situation into a good one.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"being-human","title":"Being Human","paragraphs":["“It is easy to become a scholar, but difficult to become a true human being,” we often hear. What, then, does being human mean?","It means living with virtue, wisdom, civility, justice, honor, and dignity.","Being courteous, perceptive, respectful, and humble—showing regard for others without praising oneself.","Thinking before acting, keeping a sense of balance, remaining consistent, and standing firm in adversity.","Keeping one’s word; refusing to worship money or position; neither stealing nor taking what belongs to another.","Rejecting lies and slander, never betraying others for personal gain, and defending what is right.","Remaining free of hypocrisy and hatred, refusing to turn people against one another, and knowing how to love.","Moral: True worth lies not in knowledge alone, but in humanity. A heart capable of respect also makes room for love and justice.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-self-confident","title":"Believe in Yourself","paragraphs":["Value yourself!","Do not give in to weakness.","Build on your strengths.","Do not lose hope!","Keep learning.","Be determined!","Act with good intentions.","Protect your dignity!","Be faithful!","Moral: Value yourself, do not lose hope, protect your dignity. Self-confidence is a person's strongest support.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"do-not-do-these-things","title":"Be Mindful of What You Say","paragraphs":["Do not dwell on your wealth in front of someone who is poor, your health in front of someone who is ill, or your happiness in front of someone who is suffering. Be equally thoughtful when speaking of freedom to a prisoner, strength to someone who is weak, children to someone who has none, or parents to an orphan.","Moral: Be careful not to deepen another person’s sense of loss. They need understanding, not reminders of what they lack.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dont-open-your-mouth","title":"Speak Kindly or Remain Silent","paragraphs":["If you're not going to open your mouth to say something nice, don't open it at all.","Moral: Words have value when we use them thoughtfully and for good. If you have nothing worthwhile to say, silence may be the wiser choice.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dont-say-i-couldnt-deliver-or-i-couldnt-manage","title":"Tomorrow Is Another Chance","paragraphs":["One day follows another. Some end happily; others bring disappointment. Whatever remains unfinished today, do not dwell on “I failed” or “I couldn’t do it.” Above all, do not lose hope. When the sun rises tomorrow, a whole new day will be yours.","Moral: Every day is a new page. You can't change yesterday, but how you write today is in your hands.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"education-or-character","title":"Training or Nature?","paragraphs":["A king asked his minister:","“Which matters more: training or nature?”","The minister answered without hesitation:","“Nature, Your Majesty.”","The king ordered his subjects to find the finest animal trainer in the country. When the man was brought before him, the king asked:","“How long would it take you to teach a cat to serve guests with a bowl in its paw?”","“Six months, Your Majesty.”","Six months later, the trainer returned to the palace with the cat.","“Have you taught it?” the king asked.","“Yes, Your Majesty.”","The court gathered, and the cat began serving the guests. As it approached the minister, the king asked again:","“Which matters more: training or nature?”","Before answering, the minister took a mouse from his pocket and released it. The cat immediately dropped the bowl and chased it.","Then the minister turned to the king.","“Nature, Your Majesty.”","Moral: Training may shape behavior, but an opportunity can reveal the nature beneath it. Learning matters, but character matters too.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"everything-is-in-our-own-hands","title":"It Is in Our Hands","paragraphs":["(From Indian folklore)","A boy decided to trick an old monk. He caught a bird, concealed it in his hands behind his back, and asked:","“Wise old man, is the bird I’m holding alive or dead?”","“If he says it’s alive,” the boy thought, “I’ll squeeze it to death. If he says it’s dead, I’ll open my hands and let it fly away. Either way, I’ll prove him wrong.”","The monk thought for a moment, then answered:","“My son, the answer is in your hands.”","The boy stood still and lowered his head.","The monk continued:","“War and peace, punishment and forgiveness, good and evil—all lie in our hands. Our intentions, not merely our cleverness, determine the course of our lives. Every person is capable of both good and evil. There is always a choice, and that choice is ours.”","Moral: Good and evil begin with choices. What shapes our lives is not simply how clever we are, but what we choose to do.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"explaining-ones-sorrow","title":"How We Express Sorrow","paragraphs":["Someone who explains a problem plainly seeks a solution. Someone who describes it beautifully is a writer; someone who speaks of it with love is a lover. Someone who conceals it behind a smile is a sage.","Moral: People carry their problems in different ways: some talk, some write, some keep quiet and smile. The deepest problems are often hidden in the deepest silence.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-give-up","title":"Knowing When to Walk Away","paragraphs":["There is dignity in walking away:","from those who value nothing,","those who trample on sincerity,","those who abandon their principles for gain,","from arrogant and boastful people,","those who are envious, gossipers,","those who make a habit of lying,","those who do not know what decency is,","those who betray their values,","those who turn a blind eye to ignorance,","those who despise their homeland, deny their nation,","those who forget their native language,","those who show no love or respect for parents and children.","There is dignity in walking away","from those who believe they cannot be replaced.","Moral: Stepping back from people who disregard your values can be an act of self-respect.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"he-would-not-have-given-so-little","title":"The King Would Not Give So Little","paragraphs":["A king offered a gold coin to a child he had taken a liking to, but the child refused. When the king asked why, the child said:","“My parents will ask where I stole it.”","“Tell them the king gave it to you.”","“Then they really won’t believe me. They’ll say, ‘If the king had given you money, he wouldn’t have given so little.’”","The king was so amused that he gave the child a bag of gold.","Moral: Great things begin in the family, in proper upbringing. A child who grows up honestly becomes an honest person tomorrow.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"honey-or-dry-bread","title":"Honey or Dry Bread?","paragraphs":["It is better to sit with a scholar and eat dry bread than to sit with an ignorant man and eat honey.","Moral: Your companions can lift you up or lead you astray. Plain bread shared with a wise person is worth more than honey shared with a fool.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"how-can-i-escape-my-thoughts","title":"Letting Go of Troubling Thoughts","paragraphs":["One day, a man came to visit one of the scholars of his time and asked:","“How can I escape the painful thoughts that keep me prisoner?”","Instead of answering, the scholar stood, wrapped his arms around a nearby post, and began shouting:","“Help! Free me from this post!”","The man, looking at him in surprise, asked:","“Why are you doing that? I came because I thought you were wise. The post isn’t holding you—you’re holding it. Let go, and you’ll be free!”","The scholar let go of the pole and said to the man:","“You’ve answered your own question,” the scholar said, releasing the post. “Your thoughts are not holding on to you; you are holding on to them. Let go, and you can begin to feel free.”","Moral: You yourself hold the thoughts that oppress you; it is in your hands to let them go. Change your perspective, and your burden will lighten.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-am-aware-now","title":"What I Know Now","paragraphs":["Reflections from someone who has weathered hard times:","I no longer try to make people love me. Those who do not want to will always find a reason.","I know my worth now. I do not have to prove it to everyone.","I no longer want to spend my life among the arrogant and self-satisfied.","I know that people who truly want something look for a way, while those who do not look for an excuse.","I do not confide in gossips. Anyone who shares other people’s secrets may share mine too.","I no longer try to change the minds of people determined not to listen.","I want distance from people who lie, make empty promises, and fail to keep their word.","I no longer want to be harsh or unforgiving. I value people who can acknowledge a mistake and apologize.","I know I am happiest with people around whom I can be myself.","Moral: True maturity is not about changing others, but about knowing who to let into your life and who to stay away from.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-got-busy-with-cleaning","title":"A Little Inner Housekeeping","paragraphs":["Today I did some cleaning.","I started inside myself:","in my muscles, my nerves, my veins—right down to my blood.","I cleared out everything that had been gathering in the depths.","First, the disappointments. I let them go without examining each one again.","You should have seen","how much space they had taken up.","In their place, I planted forgiveness, one seed for every disappointment.","Then I swept out jealousy.","There was more than I had realized.","Letting it go was easy, and I felt happy,","as though I had recovered something I had lost.","Thank Allah, I found no hatred or grudges in my heart.","Somehow, those had already gone.","Next came fear.","At first, I was afraid even to touch it.","I had grown so used to living with it.","How had I become accustomed to so much pain and worry?","Each day, fresh anxieties had brought new fears.","Meanwhile, I had kept putting off happiness and hope.","If only I had given them as much attention—nurtured a little hope each day, given without expecting a return, loved without conditions, and recognized the strength already within me. Perhaps then I would not have needed such a thorough clearing out.","The fears were hardest to remove.","They were tangled together like chains. Still, I let them go without reproaching myself for keeping them so long.","I knew that fighting them with anger would only draw them back.","Today I did some cleaning.","In the space I cleared, I planted joy, trust, and love.","I planted generosity, unconditional affection, and a willingness to share.","I planted a little wildness, sincerity, and forgiveness.","I planted love in every cell of my body.","I planted enthusiasm, excitement, and quiet.","My best thoughts come in quiet moments.","And I planted acceptance:","not surrender, but the willingness to see things as they are.","Moral: True cleanliness is in the heart: throwing away disappointment, fear, and resentment and planting love, forgiveness, and hope in their place. The one who cleans his inner world finds peace.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"i-would-not-trade-it-for-anything","title":"I Would Not Trade It for Anything","paragraphs":["A day,","a beautiful day,","a sunny day —","I would not trade it for anything.","For its sake, I reject war;","for its sake, I reject oppression;","for its sake, I reject lies.","I know they cannot live in the sun,","I know they cannot live side by side with love —","neither injustice,","nor fear,","nor hunger…","Necati Cumalı","Moral: On a beautiful, sunny day there is no place for strife, oppression, or lies. In the light, injustice, fear, and hunger cannot live.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"that-was-not-your-right","title":"Not Mine to Give","paragraphs":["A story from the Battle of Çanakkale tells of morphine being reserved for wounded soldiers who could survive. Among those brought to Dr. Tarık Nusret was his own son. Seeing that the boy could not be saved, he withheld the injection. Two hours later, holding his son’s lifeless body, he whispered, “Forgive me, my son. It was not mine to give you.”","Moral: Impartiality can demand a terrible personal sacrifice. In this story, a father refuses to claim for his son what has been reserved for others.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"telling-lies","title":"Lying","paragraphs":["“Do you ever lie?”","“Yes.”","“What about?”","“Only to myself. Every day I tell myself I’m stronger, more knowledgeable, and more capable than anyone else.”","“Perhaps you are.”","“No. There will always be people stronger or more capable than I am.”","“Then why say it?”","“Because it motivates me. It makes me want to learn, grow stronger, and keep improving.”","Moral: Sometimes the kind words you tell yourself — “you can do it” — become the force that drives you forward. A person is as strong as he believes in himself.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"may-your-face-always-smile","title":"May You Always Have Reason to Smile","paragraphs":["A person who sells vinegar with a smile will have more customers than a person who sells honey with a frown.","Moral: Warmth draws people to us. A smile costs nothing, yet can make all the difference.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dont-be-words-for-mouths-dust-for-feet","title":"Neither Gossip nor Dust","paragraphs":["“A tale on every tongue” means being the subject of gossip, rumors, or constant public discussion.","“Dust beneath anyone’s feet” means allowing others to humiliate, belittle, or treat you as worthless.","Moral: Protect both your reputation and your dignity. Do not give people reason to gossip about you, and do not allow anyone to treat you without respect.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"do-not-complain-about-any-day-you-have-lived","title":"Never Regret a Day","paragraphs":["Never regret a day you have lived:","Good days make you happy,","Bad days give you experience,","The worst days teach you a lesson,","The best days give you memories.","Moral: Every day offers something: happiness, experience, a lesson, or a memory. Each has its place in your life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"no-one-listens-to-constant-complainers","title":"The Same Complaint","paragraphs":["A wise man met a group who kept complaining about the same problems. He told them a joke, and everyone laughed. He repeated it, and only a few smiled. The third time, no one reacted.","Repeating a complaint without seeking a solution can have the same effect. Eventually, people stop listening.","Moral: Repetition alone changes nothing. Give your energy to finding a way forward.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"not-every-sorrow-is-told-to-people","title":"Choose Your Confidants Carefully","paragraphs":["Not every sorrow should be shared with everyone.","Some people may one day use it to hurt you.","Moral: Trust your pain to people who will treat it with care.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"other-peoples-opinions","title":"Other People's Opinions","paragraphs":["One day, a person lost the comma, and every sentence ran together. Then the period disappeared, and thoughts stretched on without an end. Next went the exclamation mark, taking joy, anger, and feeling with it. When the question mark vanished, the person stopped asking questions and accepted everything without challenge. Finally, the colon disappeared, along with the ability to explain.","At the end of life, only quotation marks remained, enclosing other people’s opinions.","Moral: When we surrender our own voice and understanding, other people’s ideas take their place. Preserve the values and roots that help you remain yourself.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"our-qualities-can-become-our-enemies","title":"When Strengths Become Weaknesses","paragraphs":["A peacock’s splendid tail can make it a target.","A nightingale’s beautiful song can lead it into a cage.","A fox’s fine fur can draw a hunter’s attention.","The qualities we prize most in ourselves can also make us vulnerable.","Moral: What gives us pride can also become a weakness. Recognize the risks that accompany your gifts.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"expressions-you-should-stay-away-from","title":"Words to Avoid","paragraphs":["Avoid these phrases, especially in the family, and never use them against your spouse:","“Don’t talk nonsense!”","This dismisses your spouse’s thoughts and feelings. Instead, try saying gently, “I see it differently” or “I don’t entirely agree.” You can disagree while showing that their opinion matters.","“Don’t exaggerate!”","This can make your spouse feel that their concerns are being dismissed. Instead, ask, “Can you tell me more about what’s worrying you and how you feel?” That opens the way to understanding.","“Why can’t you be more like their husband or wife?”","Comparing your spouse with someone else brings an outsider into your relationship and can leave your partner feeling hurt, unloved, and unable to trust you. Say clearly what you need rather than making comparisons.","“You don’t understand!”","This can sound insulting and dismissive. If you do not want to discuss a subject, say so respectfully: “Please don’t be hurt, but I’d rather not talk about this now. Could we talk about something else?”","“Don’t go there, don’t talk to her, don’t do this, don’t wear that, don’t talk like that, bring this, take that!”","Your spouse is your partner, not your subordinate. Discuss your concerns respectfully. Make requests rather than issuing orders, and remember to say “please.”","Moral: Words can hurt the person we love the most. In the family, we should use the language of respect and requests, not orders and insults.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"strength-and-sorrow","title":"Strength and Pain","paragraphs":["My God, give me strength enough for my sorrow, or sorrow no greater than I can bear.","Muhammad Fuzuli","Moral: In suffering, we may pray for greater strength or a lighter burden.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"flower-of-honesty","title":"The Flower of Honesty","paragraphs":["Before a prince could take the throne, tradition required him to marry. He invited the most beautiful young women in the country to the palace to choose a bride.","The daughter of a palace servant loved the prince and wanted to attend. Her mother tried to dissuade her, but the young woman was determined.","On the appointed day, the women arrived in their finest clothes, each hoping to impress him.","The prince gave each woman a seed. “Plant this and return in six months,” he said. “I will marry the woman who grows the most beautiful flower.” They went home full of excitement.","The servant’s daughter planted her seed and tended it carefully, but nothing sprouted. She tried everything she could and asked gardeners for advice, without success. Six months later, she returned with an empty pot. All the other women carried beautiful flowers.","The prince walked through the hall, examining every pot. Then he announced that he had chosen the servant’s daughter.","The others protested. How could he choose the only woman who had brought no flower?","The prince explained:","“She has brought me the most precious flower of all: honesty. None of the seeds I gave you could grow.”","Moral: Honesty is more valuable than any outward display. Truthfulness and a clear conscience reveal a person’s worth.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"goose-to-be-plucked","title":"The Goose to Be Plucked","paragraphs":["On a bitter winter day, a king went out in disguise with his chief vizier. Beside a stream they met an old tanner dipping hides into the icy water and beating them.","The king greeted the old man:","“Peace be upon you, venerable elder.”","“And upon you, ruler of the world,” the old man replied.","“What did you do during the six?” the king asked.","“Unless you add six to six, you cannot feed thirty-two,” the tanner replied.","The king asked again:","“Did you never rise in the night?”","“We did, Your Majesty, but others benefited.”","The king laughed and asked his last question:","“If I send you a goose, will you pluck it?”","“Certainly—and without making it cry out.”","The king and vizier walked on. The king asked:","“Did you understand our conversation?”","“No, Your Majesty,” the vizier admitted.","The king grew angry.","“Find out what it meant by this evening, or you will lose your head.”","After escorting the king home, the frightened vizier hurried back to the stream. The tanner was still at work.","“What were you and the king talking about?” he asked.","The old man looked him up and down.","“I don’t give answers for nothing. Pay me a hundred gold coins.”","The vizier handed over the coins.","“How did you recognize the king?”","“I’m a tanner. No one else could afford the fur he was wearing.”","The vizier scratched his head.","“What did you mean about adding six to six to feed thirty-two?”","The tanner collected another hundred coins before answering.","“He asked why I hadn’t earned enough during the six warm months to rest in winter. I told him I had to work all twelve months to feed the thirty-two teeth in my mouth.”","The vizier went on:","“And what did he mean about rising in the night?”","The tanner accepted another hundred coins.","“He was asking whether I had children to support me. I told him I had daughters, but they had married and now belonged to other households.”","The vizier lowered his head.","“And the goose he said he would send?”","The tanner laughed.","“That one you can work out for yourself.”","Moral: A wise word is revealed to those who understand it. Not everyone understands what they hear — some answers a person must find for themselves.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"strongest-shield","title":"The Strongest Shield","paragraphs":["The strongest shield in life is distance.","Moral: Healthy distance can protect your peace. You need not remain close to everything or everyone that hurts you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-best-things","title":"The Three Best Things","paragraphs":["A father told his son:","“In life, aim for the finest food, the finest bed, and the finest home.”","The son asked:","“But we’re poor. How can we have those?”","His father replied:","“When you’re hungry, simple food tastes wonderful. After honest work, any bed gives sweet rest. And when you make room for others in your heart, you have the finest home of all.”","Moral: The best food is found in hunger, the best sleep after hard work, and the best home is found in hearts. Happiness is not in opportunity, but in perspective.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"think-speak-and-act-positively","title":"Think, Speak, Act Positively","paragraphs":["Think positively, because your thoughts become your words.","Speak positively, because your words become your actions.","Act positively, because your actions become your habits.","Develop positive habits, because your habits become your values.","Develop positive values, because your values become your destiny.","Moral: Thoughts shape words, words shape actions, and repeated actions become habits and values. A change in outlook can begin a change in life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"try-to-think-this-way","title":"Try Another Way of Thinking","paragraphs":["Instead of “I’m no good at this,” try “What do I still need to learn? With practice, I can improve.”","Instead of “I give up,” try “Could I approach this another way?”","Instead of “This is too hard,” try “It’s difficult, but more time and effort may help.”","Instead of “This is as good as I can do,” try “There may be a way to do better.”","Instead of “I wish I hadn’t made that mistake,” try “What can it teach me?”","Instead of “I’ll never be as capable as them,” try “What can I learn from how they did it?”","Moral: Changing your mindset is the first step to changing your life. Saying “I can't” closes the door, saying “I can learn” opens up new possibilities. Failure is not the end, it is an opportunity for growth.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"turning-hardship-into-opportunity","title":"Turning Hardship into Opportunity","paragraphs":["Sometimes in life, things go very smoothly, and sometimes we face unexpected difficulties. The important thing is to be able to turn the difficulties we encounter into opportunities.","Moral: A difficulty can become a chance to learn or grow. How we respond helps shape what comes of it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"who-handles-honey-licks-his-finger","title":"Who Handles Honey Licks His Finger","paragraphs":["A beekeeper filled containers with the honey he had collected and brought them to a grocer to sell. They struck a bargain on the price and began to weigh the honey.","Because the grocer had only a one-kilo weight, he broke off pieces of honeycomb by hand and weighed the honey kilo by kilo. But before placing each piece on the scale, he never failed to lick his honey-smeared fingers.","Once, twice, three times… The beekeeper saw that some of the honey never reached the scale — it stayed on the grocer’s fingers and went to his tongue. He frowned and showed his displeasure. The grocer paid no attention and kept weighing, licking his fingers as he went.","At last the beekeeper’s patience ran out:","“You’ve licked away half my honey!”","Still licking his honeyed fingers, the grocer smiled quietly and replied:","“Don’t take offense, brother. Whoever handles honey is bound to lick a finger.”","Moral: Even a small share in a job usually brings some gain from it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"you-cannot-descend-a-well-on-his-rope","title":"Would You Trust His Rope?","paragraphs":["A ropemaker named Ali worked carelessly, producing weak ropes that often snapped and caused accidents. People began calling him “Ali of the rotten ropes.”","One day, a sheep fell into a well. A neighbor offered to climb down, but hesitated when he saw the rope the owner handed him.","“I know this rope. Ali made it. You can’t go down a well on one of his ropes.”","“Oh, you can go down,” the owner replied with a smile. “Getting back up is what I can’t promise.”","Moral: A person gains or loses trust in his name by the quality of his work. Once trust is shaken, it is very difficult to restore it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Word, silence, and communication","slug":"soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet","blurb":"The power of speech, the wisdom of silence, and right conversation.","count":12,"stories":[{"stem":"communication","title":"Communication","paragraphs":["Spend time with older people and children, and you will find experience in one and innocence in the other.","Moral: Older people offer the wisdom of experience, while children remind us of innocence. Every stage of life has something to teach us.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"tongue","title":"Language","paragraphs":["Language is a flower that blooms when we speak;","without care and nourishment, it withers.","Moral: If the language is not protected, it will wither and disappear like a flower that is not watered. Keeping your mother tongue alive means using it lovingly every day.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"silence","title":"Silence","paragraphs":["The answer to some questions is silence.","Either it's not the time for an answer,","or the person asking has not earned a reply.","* * *","Darvish Jalal Ruhavi","Moral: Sometimes silence is the wisest response. Consider both the moment and the person asking.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"silent-corridor","title":"The Silent Corridor","paragraphs":["There were two organizations working together — one was the sponsor, the other was the project development agency.","The project team worked tirelessly for months, carefully polishing the documents, and one day, when everything was ready, they sent the materials to the sponsor and began to wait for a response. Days passed, weeks passed. The result — silence.","The uncertainty within the team grew, spreading like a shadow along the corridor:","“Have the documents arrived? Have they been reviewed?”","No one knew. Each inquiry brought only another vague response.","A few months later, the team decided to go directly to the sponsor’s office to clarify the matter. There, the truth finally emerged:","“Yes, we received the documents, but we couldn’t accept them. The submission format was wrong, and the required procedure hadn’t been followed.”","The team froze in place.","“Why didn’t you tell us that?” they asked.","“We could have corrected it right away.”","Frustrated, the employees returned to the office and complained to their management:","“We should have followed up, checked the status, and made sure everyone understood the process. We’ve lost time, effort, and trust because no one kept the conversation going.”","Moral: When communication is unclear, even the best work can go unnoticed. When there is no consistent follow-up, even the strongest partnership can falter. In collaboration, the greatest enemy is often not failure—it is silence.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"saying-the-word-in-its-place","title":"The Right Words at the Right Time","paragraphs":["Choose your words well, and you will have less cause to apologize.","Moral: Thoughtful words spare regret. The right words at the right moment are precious.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"say-what-you-know","title":"Speak What You Know","paragraphs":["Share what you know without lecturing others. Listen carefully without assuming that everything you hear is true.","Silence does not prove ignorance, any more than endless talk proves wisdom. Belittling someone is foolish; excessive flattery shows a lack of courage.","Moral: Speak honestly, listen thoughtfully, and know when to be silent. See people as they are, without contempt or flattery.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"candles-conversation","title":"The Four Candles","paragraphs":["Four candles burned in a room so quiet that their voices could be heard.","The first candle said, “I am FAITH. But no one helps me keep burning. I think I will soon go out.”","Its flame dwindled until it went out altogether.","The second candle said, “I am LOYALTY. Sadly, no one seems to need me anymore. There is no reason for me to keep burning.”","As soon as it finished speaking, a gentle breeze put it out.","The third candle spoke sadly. “I am LOVE. I have no strength left to burn. People have forgotten my worth. They have even forgotten to love those closest to them.”","And with that, LOVE went out too.","Just then, a child entered the room and saw the three dark candles. “Why aren’t you burning? Weren’t you supposed to burn forever?” the child asked, beginning to cry.","The fourth candle spoke. “Don’t be afraid. As long as I am burning, we can light the others again. I am HOPE.”","The child’s eyes brightened. Taking the candle of HOPE, the child relit the others one by one.","Moral: When faith, loyalty, and love fade, hope can rekindle them. Keep hope alive, and the others can return.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"one-who-knows-and-the-one-who-does-not","title":"Knowing What You Know","paragraphs":["(Chinese Proverb)","Those who do not know and recognize it are ready to learn. Teach them.","Those who know but do not realize it are asleep. Awaken them.","Those who do not know but think they do are ignorant. Avoid their guidance.","Those who know and understand what they know can lead. Follow them.","Moral: Teach the one who yearns to learn, awaken the one who is heedless, stay away from the ignorant who thinks he knows everything, and follow the one who really knows.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"shepherds-word","title":"The Shepherd's Word","paragraphs":["Once upon a time, in a village, there lived a poor but honest shepherd named Hasan. One day, the village chief called him and said:","“Hasan, I hear you know the mountain pastures well. Look after my herd this summer. If the animals come back healthy and well fed, I’ll give you a cow.”","Hasan agreed. All summer, through rain and sunshine, he tended the herd and kept watch at night. In the fall, he brought the animals home in excellent condition.","Hasan came to the chief, hoping to receive the promised cow. The chief smiled mockingly and said:","“I never meant to give you a cow. I only said that to make you work harder. Besides, what would a poor man like you do with one?”","Hasan's eyes filled with tears. But he answered calmly and with dignity:","“You’re right that I’m poor. But I have a conscience, and I keep my word. You may have money, but if your word has no value, you are poorer than I am.”","The story spread through the village. People lost respect for the chief and avoided doing business with him. Too late, he learned that a person’s worth rests on integrity, not wealth or position.","Moral: Our true wealth lies in an honest conscience and a word others can trust.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-is-a-word","title":"What Is a Word?","paragraphs":["A word can wound;","a word can be a pearl.","Moral: A word can be both a wounding thorn and a precious pearl. What makes the difference is how you use it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"word-and-silence","title":"Words and Silence","paragraphs":["Sometimes a person says more by keeping quiet. Because words are not just sounds — they are a mirror of intention.","If a word will break a heart, if a sentence will extinguish hope — it is not worth saying.","If the words you are going to say will not warm a heart, if they will not cause a smile — be silent.","Remember... not every word blooms. Some are like thorns — they hurt the one who touches them.","Silence is wisdom. Sometimes it is the most valuable answer.","Words spoken from the heart can carry wisdom. Words spoken without thought can wound.","Silence. Because silence also speaks — sometimes more deeply, sometimes more honestly.","Moral: Not every word blooms; some hurt like thorns. Silence is wiser than words that will not bring good.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"shepherd-must-be-called","title":"Call the Shepherd","paragraphs":["After years of study, a student asked his master for a final blessing before leaving.","The master replied:","“First, answer one question. Then I’ll know whether you’ve understood what I’ve taught you.”","The student confidently says:","“Ask me, Master.”","The master said:","“Imagine you are returning to your village when a shepherd’s fierce dogs attack. How will you protect yourself?”","“I’ll throw stones to drive them away.”","“You might stop one or two, but you can’t fight the whole pack.”","“Then I’ll run.”","“They can run faster. What then?”","The student realizes that he is helpless and asks his master for help.","The master explained:","“Call the shepherd and ask him to call off his dogs. They will stop at the sound of his voice.”","Moral: Not every problem can be solved by force. Sometimes taking a step back and looking at it differently is the shortest way out.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Wisdom and life lessons","slug":"hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri","blurb":"Meaning, choices, and ways of thinking.","count":45,"stories":[{"stem":"different-path","title":"A Different Path","paragraphs":["One day, a cow returning to its shed crossed a forest where there was no path. It wandered uphill and downhill, leaving a long, winding trail behind it.","The next day, a dog came to the same place. To reach the other side of the forest, it followed the cow’s trail. Then came a flock of sheep. Seeing a ready-made path, the shepherd led the flock along it.","Before long, people began using the trail as well. They complained about its difficulty as they turned left and right, ducked beneath branches, and twisted their way through the bushes. Yet no one thought to look for a better route.","Through constant use, the trail gradually became a road. Unaware that the other side of the forest could be reached in only half an hour, people and heavily burdened animals spent three hours following the cow’s winding path.","Years passed. The small road first became the town’s main route and eventually its most important street. Although it was the least efficient route imaginable, everyone merely complained about the traffic.","Watching it all, the old and wise forest pitied the people who blindly followed the familiar road without ever asking whether it was the right one.","Moral: The path everyone follows is not always the best one. Sometimes those who step beyond habit and search for a new route reach their destination more quickly and easily.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"human-emerged","title":"A Human Being Emerged","paragraphs":["I joined thought with the heart—and love emerged.","I nurtured love through effort—and loyalty emerged.","I shared bread according to my conscience—and justice emerged.","I brought love, labor, conscience, loyalty, and justice together—and a human being emerged.","— Yaşar Kemal","Moral: When love, labor, conscience, loyalty, and justice come together, a true human being is formed. Humanity is the union of these values.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"life-lesson","title":"A Life Lesson","paragraphs":["While teaching his cub about life, a father wolf pointed to some sheep:","“Their meat is delicious.”","Then he pointed to the shepherd:","“But a blow from his staff is bitter. Be careful.”","Seeing the dog beside the shepherd, the cub exclaimed happily:","“Father, look! That one resembles us.”","The father replied:","“Run the moment you see it, my son. We have suffered most from those who look like us but are not truly one of us.”","Moral: Sometimes the greatest harm comes from those who resemble us while concealing different intentions. Recognizing hypocrisy in time is the first step toward protecting oneself.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"more-effective-way","title":"A More Effective Way","paragraphs":["A hunter walking through a forest came upon an unusual scene. A woodcutter had been hacking at a fallen tree for a long time with enormous effort. His face was drenched in sweat and his whole body was tense.","The hunter approached and asked:","“Why struggle like that? Your axe is blunt. Why don’t you sharpen it?”","The woodcutter stared at him in surprise:","“What are you talking about? Where would I find time to sharpen my axe? I still have twenty more trees to cut!”","Moral: Hard work is valuable, but unexamined effort can be wasteful. Sometimes a short pause to sharpen the tool makes all the work that follows faster and easier.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"windmill-turning-in-still-air","title":"A Windmill Turning in Still Air","paragraphs":["If a windmill turns when the air is still, someone must be blowing on its sails.","Eskimo proverb","Moral: Results have causes, even when we cannot see them. Look beyond the obvious for the forces at work.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"bookstore","title":"At the Bookstore","paragraphs":["After a long search, a medical student found the book he needed at the Bilge bookstore in Bursa. When he saw the price on the back, his smile vanished: he could not afford it.","Hiding his disappointment, he leafed through the pages, found the section assigned by his professor, and—seeing that the owners were busy with customers—read it quickly before leaving.","For the next month, the student returned every other day and studied among the shelves. One week before his final examinations, he came again, but the book was gone. He searched every shelf; it seemed to have vanished.","As the pale-faced young man was about to leave, the owners stopped him:","“Please sit down. We have something to tell you.”","One bookseller reached beneath the counter and produced the book:","“A customer asked the price recently and seemed ready to buy it. I took it off the shelf and set it aside for you. You can keep reading it here.”","After completing his studies and becoming a doctor, the young man never forgot the two booksellers. He never charged patients who could not afford treatment.","Moral: A small act of kindness can leave a lifelong mark on the heart. Those who receive kindness often carry it forward to others.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"doctors-appointment","title":"At the Doctor’s Office","paragraphs":["I was waiting for my turn at a doctor’s office. Having missed breakfast at home, I bought a sandwich and apple juice from the café and began to eat.","A boy sitting near the window was watching me closely. His expression seemed to say, “I am hungry too.” Feeling sorry for him, I returned to the café and bought him a sandwich and juice.","The boy ate eagerly, while I felt pleased with what I believed was a good deed.","At that moment, his mother emerged from the registration room. Seeing the scene, she hurried over in alarm:","“What have you done? He wasn’t supposed to eat before his appointment. I took time off work, and we’ve come such a long way. Now we’ll have to do it all again tomorrow.”","The woman could not hold back her tears. I stood frozen: while believing I was helping, I had undone an entire family’s effort.","Moral: Judgment based only on emotion and assumption can lead us to the wrong decision. Asking about the situation before helping is itself part of compassion.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"blind-who-have-eyes","title":"Eyes That Do Not See","paragraphs":["The world is full of blind people who think they see because they have eyes.","Moral: True sight requires understanding as well as eyes. We can look without seeing and listen without understanding.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"blind-well","title":"The Dry Well","paragraphs":["One day, a villager’s donkey fell into a dry well. For hours it cried out in distress. Its owner and the neighbors came to help, but after much discussion they decided there was no way to pull it out. They saw no option but to fill the well with earth. Each took a shovel and began throwing soil into the well.","The donkey shook each shovelful of earth off its back and stepped onto it. Little by little, the rising ground carried it higher, until it reached the rim and climbed out. The villagers stared in amazement as it emerged unharmed.","Moral: Shake off the burdens that fall on you and use them to climb higher. When you feel helpless, action and perseverance can reveal a way out.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"diderot-effect","title":"The Diderot Effect","paragraphs":["The eighteenth-century French writer and Enlightenment thinker Denis Diderot fell deeply into debt. News of his difficulties reached the Russian empress Catherine.","The empress offered to help by buying his library and allowing him to keep it. She also paid him twenty-five years’ salary in advance to serve as its librarian. This unexpected fortune cleared his debts and lifted a great weight from his shoulders.","Then one day, a friend gave him a luxurious red velvet robe. That was where it all began.","Diderot put on the robe and sat down to work. Suddenly, he noticed how shabby his desk looked beside the fine velvet.","Emboldened by his newfound wealth, he bought a magnificent desk to match his robe.","But now the old carpet seemed out of place beside both the robe and the desk, so he bought a new one too.","Still, something felt wrong. The armchairs, cupboards, chairs, paintings, and ornaments all looked old and mismatched.","One purchase led to another.","Before long, Diderot had replaced almost everything in the house.","Eventually he realized that his endless desire for more had brought him back to where he had started.","He wrote about the experience in an essay lamenting the loss of his old robe. Its lesson was simple:","“I was the master of my old robe, but I became the slave of the new one.”","Moral: Buying something new can make us feel compelled to replace everything around it. This is known as the Diderot effect. Desire has no natural stopping point; it is better to own modest possessions than to be ruled by luxurious ones.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-dream","title":"Dreaming","paragraphs":["Allah does not make you dream of something that He will not grant.","Moral: Trust that the dreams in your heart have meaning, and do not lose hope.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"spare-time","title":"Making Time","paragraphs":["Some people talk to you when they have time to spare. Others make time to talk to you.","Moral: Time is the only wealth that never comes back. Appreciate the person who willingly spends it for you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"from-the-memoirs-of-a-lady-from-istanbul","title":"An Istanbul Woman Remembers","paragraphs":["It was 1919. Istanbul was completely under British occupation. I had just finished high school.","I was a pretty young woman, and matchmakers often visited our home. One day, a handsome, tall young man came with his representatives. He introduced himself as a lawyer. I liked him, and we became engaged. I loved him and dreamed of making a happy home. I stayed up by lamplight, weaving little towels for my dowry.","One day, a rumor spread in the neighborhood.","“They say Ayesha’s fiancé isn’t a lawyer at all. He spends his days carrying coffins outside the mosque.”","I was devastated. My father and I went to the mosque, watched from a distance, and saw that he was really carrying a coffin.","Heartbroken, I ended the engagement.","Five years passed. I married someone else and had a child. Our country was free again.","One day I met him in the city. My son was with me.","He stopped when he saw me. He said hesitantly, “If you have time, I would like to offer you a cup of tea.” I agreed. We went into an office.","It was a law firm; his name was written on the sign on the door. His assistants were working inside.","“Are you really a lawyer?” I asked.","“Yes,” he said.","“But if you are a lawyer, why were you carrying a coffin in front of the mosque?”","He bowed his head and said, “Forgive me.”","“Istanbul was under occupation. British soldiers were everywhere, searching people and inspecting everything. We disguised arms shipments as funerals, sending coffins full of weapons to the National Forces in Anatolia. It was vital work. I couldn’t tell you.”","Moral: Don’t judge by appearance; behind every behavior there may be a truth that you don’t know. Our freedom is owed to the sacrifice of the selfless.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hayats-life-story","title":"Hayat’s Life Story","paragraphs":["When my mother left our home, my father, and us, I was six and my brother was eight. Even at that age I understood why she left my father, but I could not understand why she left us. Would a mother abandon her children?","My father used to beat my mother. He beat me and my brother too. I came to believe that all men beat their wives and children…","After my mother left, home became four walls. No one called us to wash our faces because breakfast was ready. No one even asked whether we were hungry, though we might go days without food. The hardest lesson was this: without the comfort of a mother’s presence, a child can grow up overnight. I grew up at six.","About ten days after my mother left, the police came to our house. They said she had taken her own life. In her hand she had been clutching an envelope. On it was written: “To be given to my daughter and my son.”","I could not read then — how was I supposed to read it? My brother read it. I cried as I listened. My brother cried too.","My mother had gone to her father’s house, but he flew into a rage. In his eyes, a woman had to stay with her husband. Even with a mouth full of blood, she was expected to say, “I’ve been drinking red sherbet.” My mother begged him: “Let me stay for a month. Then I’ll find a way to take my children and start again.”","But a woman living alone? To my grandfather, that would disgrace the family. He would never be able to hold his head up again; he said he would rather see his daughter dead. That evening, my mother took her own life. My grandmother told me this years later, on her deathbed, when I was nineteen.","When my father heard of my mother’s death, he was not upset at all. He said he would put us in an orphanage. From then on we would live there; that would be our home. Brother and sister might be parted, forget each other, and years later fail to know each other — so we were both to keep Mother’s letter.","But before I could ask how the two of us would keep one letter, my brother cut it in half with scissors. He gave me the beginnings of the sentences. Since I had the openings, once I learned to read I would have to guess the rest. My brother had already memorized the letter anyway.","When my aunt heard we were to be sent to an orphanage, she came. She told my father: “A girl is not given to an orphanage. I will take Hayat and raise her myself.” Living with my aunt, I understood why: a girl meant an unpaid servant ordered about the house. Not once did my aunt call me by my name. My name became “Come-here”: “Come-here, fetch water… Come-here, wash these dishes…”","Once a month my brother visited me at my aunt’s. He said he was fine in the orphanage. I said I was fine too — I did not want to upset him. Did he also say “I’m fine” so I would not worry? I never dared ask.","I had started school. I was the first in the class to learn to read. How could I not — I was going to read my mother’s letter. The part I still had began: “Hayat, my beautiful daughter, I…” Every night in bed I tried to finish that sentence:","“Hayat, my beautiful daughter, I love you so much.”","“Hayat, my beautiful daughter, I miss you terribly.”","“Hayat, my beautiful daughter, I am waiting for you.”","I built a different dream around each ending. In those dreams, I was happy. Who chooses to dream of sorrow?","I finished middle school. My aunt said I would not go to school anymore. But I wanted to study — to stand on my own feet and live under one roof with my brother.","That summer the wife of Uncle Hasan, the neighbourhood grocer with three children, died of cancer. I was heartbroken. What would the three children do — how would they find a mother’s scent? Why did mothers die? Would those three forget their names as I had, and become “Come-here” too?","While I was grieving for Uncle Hasan’s children, it came out that I was betrothed to him. It turned out I had been sold for eight bracelets and three thousand liras. Because I was too young for a civil marriage, forty days later our religious marriage was performed.","I was fifteen. I was Uncle Hasan’s wife. I had three children — two, five, and six. And my aunt insisted we must have a child of our own… I was supposed to have a child so my place would be secure and Uncle Hasan would not look at other women.","The first slap from Uncle Hasan came because I called him “Uncle Hasan.” Does a woman call her husband “uncle”? When I lost my mother at six, I learned to keep silent. So I did not ask, “Does a man call a fifteen-year-old girl his wife?” — I stayed quiet.","When Uncle Hasan hit me, all three children clung to their father’s legs: “Don’t hit Sister Hayat — she plays with us, she tells us stories,” they begged. I was those children’s older sister. What I called stories were my dreams.","When Uncle Hasan went out, I practiced saying “Hasan” in front of the mirror. But every time I said it, I pictured his thinning hair, his glasses slipping down his nose, and his large belly. Calling him by his name made me feel ashamed, as though I were doing something wrong. One day, his six-year-old son heard me. “Sister Hayat, Mother used to call him ‘Bey,’” he said. “You could call him that too.”","“Bey” — of course, “Bey” would be better. I bent and kissed dark-eyed Sami, who listened to my dreams as if they were fairy tales. I called five-year-old Elif and two-year-old Zahra and began to tell them a story. That day I began: “Once upon a time there was a six-year-old girl named Nagil. Nagil had lost her mother. She searched everywhere; when she could not find her, she went to the land of dreams. There Nagil was so happy that she never returned to the real world.”","In my tale Nagil was always happy, always smiling. Every day I told the children that story. We were very happy.","Uncle Hasan was good too. I now called him “Bey.” From time to time he grew angry, but I understood why: instead of a woman he had bought, for eight bracelets and three thousand liras, a storytelling sister.","My brother came to visit. Each time he wanted to give me his half of Mother’s letter. I would not take it. I was afraid that if I read the other half, I would fall from the land of dreams into a cruel real world and be unhappy. All my dreams were built on that other half.","By the time I was forty, the children who had listened to my stories were grown. Sami had become a doctor and moved to another city. Whenever he felt low, he called and said, “Sister Hayat, tell me a story.”","Elif had become a teacher and married. I had a granddaughter. They badly wanted to name her Hayat; I would not allow it. Elif said, “Then let her name be Nagil.” So my granddaughter’s name was Nagil.","My youngest, Zahra, became a veterinarian. “Sister Hayat, whenever an animal is restless I tell it a story, and it calms down,” she would say. Zahra married too, and I had a grandson.","When I was forty-five, Uncle Hasan—my Bey—died. I grieved for him. His last words were a plea for forgiveness, and I forgave him with all my heart. I remembered the good in him.","But there are others I cannot forgive:","against those who see no wrong in marrying off fifteen-year-old girls, and those who support them;","those who call a daughter’s wish to escape daily beatings a disgrace, and those who stand behind them;","against those who show no mercy to children left helpless and hopeless like wounded birds, and those who support them —","These people I cannot forgive.","Moral: Blind custom and a false idea of “honor” can destroy a woman’s life, a child’s life. Those who look away from early marriage, violence, and neglect are partners in that oppression.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"if-i-dont-do-it-these-days-the-world-wont-collapse","title":"The World Will Not Fall Apart","paragraphs":["When I asked an employee how he was progressing with a task I had assigned him, his reply disappointed me deeply.","“The world won’t fall apart if I don’t do it this week.”","True, I thought, the world would not fall apart. But what about our trust? What would that attitude do to our working relationship, our mutual respect, and the work we shared?","A delay of a few days was not the real issue. It was his attitude toward a commitment. Responsibility includes keeping your word and giving others reason to rely on you.","I felt that his dismissive answer showed little regard for my time, effort, or trust.","I admit that it hurt. I knew then that we could not continue working together in quite the same way. A professional relationship depends on trust, and ours had been shaken.","Most disappointing of all, he seemed to recognize that his response was wrong, yet neither apologized nor completed the task. I had to find another way to get the work done.","The experience reminded me that an organization’s most valuable asset is not time, money, or technology, but the trust people place in one another. It can take years to build and a single sentence to damage.","Moral: Trust is built by keeping commitments. What takes years to earn can be lost in a moment of carelessness.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"if-the-door-before-you-wont-open-it-is-not-your-door","title":"When One Door Closes","paragraphs":["Jamal heard that a promotion might be available and went to speak to his manager, Salim Bey. He hoped he would be considered. Salim Bey said he was satisfied with Jamal’s work, but then unexpectedly dismissed him. “You’re not the right fit for us,” he said. Jamal left deeply disappointed.","Two years later, they met again and exchanged friendly greetings. Jamal said he had started his own company and that business was going well. Salim Bey was delighted.","“In fact, I owe you thanks,” Jamal said. “You let me go instead of keeping me somewhere I didn’t belong. That gave me the push to do work I truly enjoy. I’m very happy now.”","Moral: A closed door can lead to a better path. Losing one opportunity sometimes creates room for another.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"it-is-in-vain","title":"Wasted Effort","paragraphs":["It is futile to advise a fool, to argue with an ignorant person, to befriend a hypocrite.","Moral: Choosing your company helps shape your life. Do not waste it arguing with people unwilling to learn or trusting those who are insincere.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"it-wont-go-to-waste","title":"It Won’t Go to Waste","paragraphs":["A kindness done from the heart,","an act shaped by love,","a word spoken in sincerity,","behavior guided by courtesy,","a smile warmed by affection,","a step taken for truth and justice,","respect shown to moral values —","never goes to waste. It lights the way for others and makes your own life richer and brighter.","Moral: Kindness, words, and deeds from the heart never vanish; they light others’ way and brighten your life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"lecturer-and-the-groom","title":"The Lecturer and the Stablehand","paragraphs":["A lecturer arrived in a village to give a talk. Only one man sat in the hall. After greeting him, the lecturer asked, “No one else has come. Should I give the talk anyway?”","The man replied:","“Sir, I look after twenty horses. If nineteen wandered off and only one remained, I would still feed and water that one.”","Encouraged, the lecturer began. He spoke at great length, then asked:","“Well, how did you like it?”","“I’m only a stablehand, and I didn’t understand much,” the man replied. “But I can tell you this: I wouldn’t give one horse enough feed and water for twenty.”","Moral: Good speaking is not measured by length. What matters is whether your listener can understand and absorb what you say.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"life-is-short","title":"Life Is Short","paragraphs":["Life is short. Spend it with people who bring you happiness and make you feel loved.","Moral: Give your time to relationships that bring love and joy into your life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"liver","title":"The Liver in Language","paragraphs":["Why does the word for “liver” appear in expressions such as “jan-jiyar,” for a close and trusted friend; “jiyarguşam,” for a beloved child; “agqıyar,” for a coward; and “jiyarparam,” for someone dearly loved?","The liver’s warmth and rich blood supply impressed ancient peoples. They associated warm blood with life and its absence with death, and came to regard the liver as a center of life.","The Babylonians, for example, believed the soul resided there. In some cultures, the liver represented emotion before the heart took that role in expressions and literature.","In ancient Greece, virtue and related qualities such as courage and determination were attributed to the liver. If a person had a healthy liver, he was considered virtuous—he was perceived as someone who followed his principles, overcoming all obstacles.","Moral: Language does not arise by chance; behind every word there is a history, a belief. Our words are the memory of our past.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"living-in-the-past","title":"Living in the Past","paragraphs":["Living in the past is like walking backward. With your eyes turned away from the road ahead, you stumble—unable either to change what lies behind you or to reach what lies before you.","Moral: You cannot undo the past. Turn toward the future and give yourself a chance to move forward.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"newtons-second-law","title":"Newton’s Second Law","paragraphs":["A physics teacher returned to the staff room after class. “These students are hopeless,” he complained. “I explained Newton’s second law three times. By the third time, even I understood it—but they still didn’t!”","Moral: Before teaching a subject, make sure you understand it yourself. Repetition alone cannot replace clarity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"nothing-is-ever-truly-lost","title":"Nothing Is Ever Truly Lost","paragraphs":["A setback need not be a complete loss. Sometimes you succeed; sometimes you learn.","Moral: Learn from failure, and the experience has not been wasted.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"pawn-and-the-king","title":"The Pawn and the King","paragraphs":["Do not look down on a pawn.","One day, it may become a queen.","Do not feel safe merely because you are a king.","One day, you may be checkmated.","Moral: Do not underestimate the humble or place too much faith in rank. A pawn can rise, and a king can fall.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"sowing-millet-at-the-bottom","title":"The Saplings That Bore Gold","paragraphs":["One day, the king went on a walk with his courtiers. In a village on the way, he saw an old man planting seedlings in his field. He called out to the elder from a distance:","“Old father, why plant saplings at your age? You may never taste their fruit.”","The elder replied to the king:","“That doesn’t matter, my son. We ate the fruit of trees planted before our time. Those who come after us will eat from these.”","This answer pleased the king and he ordered the elder to be given a bag of gold.","The elder pointed to the bag full of gold and said:","“You see? My trees have already borne fruit.”","Delighted, the king ordered another bag of gold. The shrewd old farmer smiled.","“Most trees bear fruit once a year. Mine have already borne fruit twice.”","The king ordered a third bag, but the vizier stepped forward.","“Your Majesty, we should leave. At this rate, he’ll empty the treasury and plant millet on its floor!”","Moral: A person does not live only for himself. We ate what those who came before us planted; we must plant for those who come after us.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"properties-of-water","title":"What Water Teaches Us","paragraphs":["Consider water:","It nourishes a tree.","It puts out a fire.","It washes away dirt.","Mixed with flour, it makes dough.","Meeting sunlight, it forms a rainbow.","Flowing, it brings life.","Standing still, it can grow stagnant.","The heart is like water: it is renewed through connection, but can grow stagnant in isolation.","Moral: Human connection keeps the heart alive, just as movement keeps water fresh.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"teacher-hello-do-you-remember-me","title":"Do You Remember Me?","paragraphs":["“Hello! Do you remember me? You taught me at school.”","“Yes, in primary school. How good to see you after so many years. What do you do now?”","“I’m a teacher, like you.”","“Wonderful. What made you choose teaching?”","“You did. I wanted to help children grow and find confidence, as you helped me.”","“I’m glad to hear that. Was there something in particular?”","“One boy in our class had a beautiful watch. My parents couldn’t afford one, and I wanted it badly. During a break, I stole it from his pocket. When he reported it missing, you asked for it back. No one answered. You closed the door and told all the boys you would search their pockets. I thought my life was over.","“You lined us up and asked us to cover our eyes. After finding the watch in my pocket, you kept searching everyone else. Then you returned it without saying who had taken it. You never mentioned it to me or my parents. You let me learn from what I’d done without being labeled a thief for the rest of my childhood.","“That taught me what a teacher can be: someone who guides a child through a mistake while protecting their dignity. I decided to become that kind of teacher.”","The teacher listened and was astonished. The young man asked:","“Don’t you remember finding the watch in my pocket?”","“I remember the incident, but I didn’t know you had taken it.”","“How could you not know? You found it yourself!”","“I had closed my eyes too.”","Moral: To protect a person’s dignity without shaming them — by closing your eyes — is true teaching. A person whose dignity is kept learns from the mistake and rises.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"dead-remain-in-life","title":"The One Left Behind","paragraphs":["When two people love each other wholeheartedly and one dies, the survivor may feel that they, too, have ceased to live.","Moral: The person left behind carries both love and longing. Separation can make the depth of that love painfully clear.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"most-beautiful-places-in-the-world","title":"The Most Beautiful Places","paragraphs":["The most beautiful places to be are in the thoughts, prayers, and hearts of those you love.","Moral: Our dearest place is not always somewhere on a map. It may be in a loved one’s heart.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"road-to-the-cotton-field","title":"The Road Beyond the Cotton Fields","paragraphs":["We could barely read or write when a new teacher came to our village. Aydin became more than a teacher to us; he was like a brother and a father. Seeing our dilapidated school and learning that we expected to spend our lives in the cotton fields brought tears to his eyes.","He asked where one village road led. “To the cotton fields,” we answered. Then he pointed to another road. None of us knew where it went. We had never left the village and knew little of the world beyond it.","That day, he also asked us what we wanted to be when we grew up. We all remained silent because we didn't know what to answer...","He soon taught us to read and write. Then one day, he brought letters from people he said would be our pen pals: doctors, engineers, scientists, and others. For years, these strangers wrote about their work, the meaning of life, and the possibilities opened by education. Until then, none of us had imagined a future beyond growing cotton.","We had believed there was only one road open to us: the road to the fields.","The letters changed that. We began to see where the other road might lead. With Aydin’s help, we continued our studies. Some of us trained as doctors, others as engineers or teachers.","Years later, another letter came to us:","“My children, never stop dreaming. Work hard and keep moving toward your goals. I congratulate you with all my heart. I always knew you could succeed.”","“Your pen pal, Aydin.”","We were astonished. Our own teacher had written all those letters that changed our lives.","Moral: A teacher not only showed the way to children, but also created new paths for them. For a person who can dream, the world no longer consists of just one path.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"secret-of-living-well-and-longevity","title":"The Secret of a Long and Happy Life","paragraphs":["Halve your portions, double your walks, triple your laughter, and place no limit on your love.","Tibetan proverb.","Moral: Eat little, walk a lot, laugh a lot, love endlessly. The secret to a long and happy life lies in simplicity and love.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"no-need-to-be-sane-when-everyone-is-mad","title":"Sane in a World Gone Mad","paragraphs":["A wise man received a warning from above: “Tomorrow the water will change, and everyone who drinks it will lose their reason.” He hurried home and filled every jug and bowl he owned. The next day, the warning came true. For a week or two, he drank only his stored water. At last, unable to endure his isolation, he ventured outside. But everyone he spoke to mocked him. “Are you mad? You belong in an asylum!” Despairing, he returned home, smashed his containers, drank from the same fountain as everyone else, and went to bed.","Moral: The crowd may condemn you for knowing the truth. Protect your mind, but don't tire yourself out by trying to prove your worth to the public every moment.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"this-boat-is-empty-too","title":"This Boat Is Empty Too","paragraphs":["My master used to say:","“When I was young, I often went to the lake to think in solitude. I had a little boat and would spend hours on the water, letting my thoughts settle.","“One morning at daybreak, I was sitting there with my eyes closed.","“Suddenly, another boat struck mine. Furious at the interruption, I opened my eyes, ready to shout at whoever was responsible. But the boat was empty. With no one to blame, my anger faded. I closed my eyes again, and by sunrise my peace had returned.","“That empty boat taught me something. Now, when someone upsets me, I tell myself:","“‘This boat is empty too.’”","Moral: If a person wants to maintain his peace, he should not look for evil intentions behind every incident. Sometimes what worries us is just a coincidence; it is our choice whether to magnify it and turn it into anger.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-questions","title":"Three Questions","paragraphs":["The king called the vizier to him and said:","“Answer three questions by tomorrow or leave the palace. What does Allah patiently bear? What does He conceal? And what does He bring about through His wisdom and power?”","The vizier thought hard but found no answers. He returned home in despair. His clever servant noticed his distress and asked what was wrong.","The servant said:","“I know the answers,” the servant said. “I’ll give you two now and the third tomorrow. Allah bears His servants’ sorrows and conceals their faults.”","The next day, the vizier went to the king and told him these two answers. The king said:","“Correct. Did you think of these answers yourself?”","The vizier answered honestly:","“My servant taught me.”","The king ordered the vizier to hand his robe to the servant. The two exchanged clothes and positions.","Stunned, the former vizier asked:","“And the third answer? What does Allah bring about through His wisdom and power?”","The servant smiled.","“Haven’t you seen? In a moment, He can make a vizier a servant and a servant a vizier.”","Moral: Position is never permanent. Remember that circumstances can change, and remain humble.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-landscapes","title":"Three Scenes","paragraphs":["On a journey, a young man witnessed three puzzling scenes.","First, he saw people sowing wheat that sprang up and ripened as soon as it touched the soil. Yet instead of harvesting it, the farmers burned the field.","Next, he saw a man struggling to lift a stone. Only after adding a second stone could he raise it. With a third, it became easier still. Bewildered, the traveler continued.","Finally, he saw a man riding a sheep, with others clinging to his shoulders. The sheep ran so swiftly that the people chasing it could not catch up.","At a city gate, he met a radiant old man and told him what he had seen. The elder explained:","“The farmers represent people who do good, then destroy its value by constantly boasting about it.","“The stones represent sin. The first wrongdoing weighs heavily. But without repentance, the next becomes easier, and the one after that easier still.","“The sheep is an animal of paradise, carrying its riders there. The first rider represents scholars, and those holding on are believers from every walk of life. Those left chasing behind are people without faith.”","Moral: The three scenes warn against boasting of good deeds and growing accustomed to wrongdoing, while honoring the value of wise spiritual guidance.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-statues","title":"The Three Statues","paragraphs":["Two neighboring rulers were close friends. For one ruler’s birthday, the other commissioned three identical gold statues, each an inch high. A hidden difference between them was known only to him and the sculptor.","The statues were ready and were sent to the neighboring ruler on his birthday. The letter accompanying the gift said: “I congratulate you on your birthday with these three golden statues. At first glance, the statues may seem exactly the same. But one of them is more valuable than the other two. When you find that statue, let me know.”","The recipient weighed the statues, but each weighed exactly the same. Further examination revealed nothing.","He summoned experts from across the country. They studied the statues for days without solving the puzzle.","At last, a man asked for a fine wire. He passed it into the first statue’s ear; it emerged from the other ear. In the second, it came out through the mouth. In the third, it stayed inside. The difference was clear.","The ruler writes a reply to his neighbor who sent the statues and sends it:","“These statues represent three kinds of people:","those who let what they hear go in one ear and out the other;","those who immediately repeat it;","and those who keep it in their hearts.","“The third is the most valuable: a person who keeps a confidence and knows when it is right to speak. Thank you for this thoughtful gift.”","Moral: The most valuable person is the one who can keep what he hears as a secret in his heart and only speaks when it is appropriate. Keeping your word is also a form of wisdom.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"to-be-full-or-to-be-gone","title":"Sharing and Division","paragraphs":["By sharing, we are nourished.","By division, we are lost.","Yunus Emre","Moral: Sharing strengthens us; division weakens us. Unity needs care and a shared sense of purpose.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"searching","title":"Searching in the Wrong Place","paragraphs":["To search for something where it cannot be found is to deceive yourself—like looking for loyalty in betrayal, decency in shamelessness, or mercy in dishonesty.","Moral: Do not expect everyone to offer what they do not possess. See people clearly, rather than through your hopes for them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"two-bowls-of-water","title":"Two Cups of Water","paragraphs":["A wealthy ruler liked to travel with some of his treasure and show it off. One day, he asked his trusted teacher, “What do you think of my power and riches?”","“Imagine you are dying of thirst in a desert,” the teacher said. “Would you give half your wealth for a cup of water?”","“Of course.”","“And if the thirst returned, would you give the other half for another cup?”","“To stay alive, yes.”","“Then do not boast too much,” the teacher said. “All your wealth is worth just two cups of water.”","Moral: The value of riches depends on what we need. In a desert, water can be worth more than a treasury.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-do-i-need-it-for","title":"What Is It to Me?","paragraphs":["Suleiman the Magnificent (1494–1566), who ruled from 1520 until his death, brought the Ottoman state to great power. Yet he sometimes wondered whether the dynasty might one day decline and fall.","He wrote to his foster brother, the scholar Yahya Efendi, whose spiritual insight he respected. “Under what circumstances does a state collapse? Could such a fate await the house of Osman?”","The reply was brief: “What is it to me, my Sultan?”","Reading it at Topkapı Palace, Suleiman was bewildered. Surely Yahya Efendi would not dismiss so serious a question. He went to see him in Beşiktaş. “Brother, please take my letter seriously and give me an answer.”","Yahya Efendi:","“I have, my Sultan. I considered it carefully and gave you my answer.”","“But all you said was, ‘What is it to me?’ You seem to want nothing to do with the matter.”","Yahya Efendi explained:","“When oppression spreads and injustice takes the throne, people ask, ‘What is it to me?’ The shepherds begin devouring the sheep they should protect. Those who know the truth conceal it. The cries of the poor rise to heaven, but no one listens. Then the end is near. The treasury empties, trust disappears, and respect for public order crumbles. That is how a state falls.”","Moved to tears, the sultan bowed his head. He left grateful that someone in his country would speak so plainly to him.","Moral: A society weakens when people see injustice and say, “What is it to me?” Indifference allows oppression to grow.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-it-means-to-be-late","title":"Before It Is Too Late","paragraphs":["Sometimes being late means more than missing an appointment. It means missing a chance to do what matters in a relationship.","Expressing love, accepting affection, forgiving someone, or offering an apology all matter while there is still time.","We all make mistakes. What matters is acknowledging them, making amends, and not delaying the words that need to be said. Life is short.","Let us live with more reasons to say “I’m glad I did” and fewer reasons to say “If only.”","Moral: Expressing love, forgiving, and apologizing on time is beautiful. Life is short - let us not postpone the affairs of the heart.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-matters-in-life","title":"What Matters Most","paragraphs":["A teacher entered her classroom and placed a glass jar on the desk.","“We have only one life,” she said. “Using our time wisely helps us make the most of it.” She filled the jar with golf balls. “Is it full?”","“Yes,” the students answered.","She added a handful of pebbles, which settled between the balls.","“What about now?”","“Yes.”","Then she poured sand into the remaining gaps.","“And now?”","The students confirm that the jar is full this time.","She explained:","“Think of this jar as your life.","“The golf balls are what you value most: your family, friends, and loved ones.","“The pebbles are other important things, such as your work and possessions.","“The sand is everything else—the smaller concerns.","“If I put the sand in first, there would be no room for the balls or pebbles. Fill your life with small matters, and you may leave no time or energy for what matters most.”","Moral: Fill the jar of life with the most valuable things first—family, love, and health. If you get caught up in the little things, there will be no room for the important things.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-we-learn-in-life","title":"What Life Teaches Us","paragraphs":["Throughout life, we keep learning from others:","some teach us patience, love, loyalty, and integrity; others show us what deceit, malice, and betrayal look like.","Moral: Every encounter can teach us something—either how to behave or how not to.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"where-does-this-road-go","title":"Where Does This Road Go?","paragraphs":["A young traveler asked an old man sitting beside the road:","“Where does this road go?”","“Nowhere, my son.”","“How can a road go nowhere?”","“I’ve lived here fifty years, and it hasn’t moved. It’s the people who come and go.”","Moral: The road stays where it is. The important thing is knowing where you want to go.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Work, livelihood, and wealth","slug":"emek-ruzi-ve-servet","blurb":"Labor, provision, and our view of wealth.","count":19,"stories":[{"stem":"organization-without-an-action-plan","title":"An Organization Without an Action Plan","paragraphs":["An organization without an action plan is like a ship that knows its destination but has no chart, no chosen route, and no assigned duties for its crew. It may reach port through luck, a skillful captain, or favorable weather. But lasting success needs more than fortunate circumstances: it requires careful planning and consistent action.","Over time, an organization without a plan also begins to lose its members. People are sustained not only by ideas, but by the hope of seeing those ideas put into practice. When hope fades, people leave; when people leave, the organization gradually weakens and eventually ceases to function.","Moral: A goal provides direction; a plan defines the path toward it. An organization may advance by chance without a plan, but lasting success requires deliberate and consistent action.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"fish-shop","title":"At the Fish Shop","paragraphs":["After work, a man was walking toward the bus stop when he passed a fish shop. Remembering how much his wife and daughter loved fish, he went inside to surprise them with dinner.","He asked the seller for one kilogram, then changed the order to two. At that moment, an elderly woman in a worn coat approached timidly:","“Son, would you buy some fish for me too?”","Without hesitation, the man told the seller:","“Make it four kilograms—two for me and two for her.”","He then asked whether she had bread. When she said no, he hurried to the shop across the street, bought four loaves, and gave her a share.","“Is there anything else you need?”","The woman took an empty medicine bottle from her pocket. They were eye drops she had last been able to buy two months earlier; without them, her sight was worsening.","The man went to the pharmacy. The fish seller, who had watched everything, offered the woman a chair. Returning with the medicine, the man was heartened by the seller’s compassion as well.","He gave the woman the medicine and then all the money left in his pocket. Blessing him, she made her way home.","As the man headed toward the bus stop, he remembered that he had not paid for the fish. His pockets were empty. The seller simply smiled:","“Go on, brother. The fish is my gift.”","With no bus fare, the man walked home. He called his daughter, told her he had bought fish, and promised to arrive soon. As he walked through the rain, he thanked Allah for the chance to help; the tears on his cheeks mingled with the rainwater.","Moral: Do not wait for great means before doing good; kindness depends not on wealth, but on a generous heart. A person rich in spirit finds a way to help even while possessing little.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"baklava","title":"Baklava","paragraphs":["When I was a child, our family had very little money. One day, my father brought home some baklava, and we enjoyed it with tea that evening.","The next day was a holiday. My mother packed one piece of baklava, a few olives, and some bread for me to take to school.","My classmates had many different foods. A boy I disliked saw my modest meal and laughed:","“Did you bring that baklava for the mice?”","When the whole class laughed, I ran home in tears and shouted at my mother:","“Why did you give me only one piece? Everyone else had so much food—you humiliated me!”","My mother answered sadly:","“My child, that was my own piece.”","Her words broke my heart. She had saved her only piece of baklava for me instead of eating it herself. I was too ashamed even to hug her. Ever since, I have asked for forgiveness whenever I remember how I behaved. I have never been able to eat baklava again.","Moral: Mocking another person’s poverty is among the gravest wrongs. Never forget that a mother may surrender her own share—even her final bite—for her child.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"chief-accountant-and-the-ceo","title":"The Chief Accountant and the CEO","paragraphs":["The chief accountant asked the CEO:","“What if we spend time and money training our employees, and then they leave?”","The CEO replied:","“What if we don’t train them, and they stay?”","Moral: Investing in people is not a waste. Saving a little on their development today can lead to a much greater loss tomorrow.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"do-you-have-spending-money","title":"Do You Have Enough Money?","paragraphs":["Value your parents while they are alive. Instead of merely asking, “Do you have enough money?” do what you can to meet their needs. No one can take their place.","Moral: Show your parents that you value them through practical care. No one and nothing can replace them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"everyone-has-work-to-do","title":"Everyone Has Work to Do","paragraphs":["I have been fortunate to learn from wise teachers. One was my literature teacher, the esteemed Baladadash. He taught us never to leave a request or an assigned task unanswered, however small it might seem.","Everyone has things to do. When someone asks for help, it is usually because they need you, not because they have nothing better to do. Do not leave them waiting in silence. If you cannot help, say so openly. Failing to answer erodes their trust.","Moral: It is a very painful feeling to live knowing you are regarded as unreliable and untrustworthy.","Bakhtiyar Sirajov"],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"former-minister-at-the-seminar","title":"The Former Minister’s Cup","paragraphs":["A former minister was invited to speak at a seminar. He stepped up to the lectern holding a plastic cup, but seemed distracted. Raising the cup, he took a deep breath and addressed the audience.","“Last year I spoke from this same lectern,” he said. “The difference is that I was a minister then. I flew business class. A limousine and escort cars met me at the airport and took me to a luxury hotel suite. The next morning, the same limousine brought me here. There was a special welcome, an expensive porcelain cup of coffee, and a reserved seat. Yesterday, I paid for my own flight.","“No one met me at the airport. I took a taxi to the hotel and found my own room. This morning, they checked my name on a list and let me in. I found a seat myself. When I asked for coffee, someone pointed to a machine in the hallway. That’s where I filled this plastic cup.”","The audience laughed. “Last year’s porcelain cup wasn’t for me,” he continued. “It was for my position.” The room broke into applause. When it quieted, he added:","“That taught me something. The praise, privileges, and special treatment that come with office belong to the position, not to the person holding it. Once you leave, the porcelain cup becomes a plastic one. That is what you are entitled to, like everyone else.”","Moral: The respect shown to you is often for your position, not for yourself. Therefore, do not brag about your position — your true value will be seen when you leave.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"garden-and-the-gardener","title":"The Garden and the Gardener","paragraphs":["A teacher said to his students:","“Picture a gardener tending a garden. Can you see it?”","“Yes,” they replied.","The teacher says in a serious voice:","“Remember this: it would be wrong to say the garden grew entirely because of the gardener. But it would be just as wrong to say it grew entirely by itself.”","Moral: Recognize both your own efforts and the help you receive. No one grows entirely alone or entirely through the work of others.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"grocery-shop-and-the-supermarket","title":"The Little Shop and the Supermarket","paragraphs":["An elderly woman ran a small grocery store in our neighborhood. When a supermarket opened opposite, both shops began advertising prices outside. The supermarket put up a sign: “Butter: 70 dollars.” The woman’s sign read “Butter: 60 dollars.” The next day, the supermarket lowered its price to 50. She promptly advertised 40.","One of the residents of the neighborhood, concerned about this situation, turned to the woman:","“Auntie, you shouldn’t compete with them. They can afford these price cuts. You’ll go out of business.”","The woman replied without losing her composure:","“I don’t sell butter,” she replied calmly. “I just want people to be able to buy it cheaply.”","Moral: A person who lives by the question “Whom did I help?” rather than “What did I gain?” is different. The one who gives without expecting anything in return is actually the one who gains the most.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"henry-fords-choice","title":"Henry Ford's Choice","paragraphs":["It is said that Henry Ford once invited two young friends to dinner. Both hoped to work for his company, and both were talented graduates of the same university.","After the meal, Ford offered one a job and wished the other well. The disappointed young man gathered his courage and asked:","“Mr. Ford, we haven’t discussed cars or engineering all evening. We only talked about everyday things. Why did you offer my friend a job and turn me down?”","Ford replied:","“For two reasons. First, I watched you both during dinner. Your friend tasted his food before adding salt. You added salt before tasting it. I value people who examine something before deciding to change it.","“Second, your friend treated the waiters courteously. You ignored them and were polite only to me. Everyone deserves respect, whatever their position.”","Moral: Small actions reveal character, both in how we make decisions and in how we treat people with less power than ourselves.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"his-hand-is-at-work","title":"At Least I Look Busy","paragraphs":["A man bought ten eggs for ten kopecks and sold them for nine. When acquaintances asked the purpose of this strange trade, he replied:","“Never mind the loss. As long as people see me trading, they think I’m a merchant. At least everyone can see that I’m working.”","Moral: Wasting time on empty work for the sake of appearances is the real loss. Looking busy does not bring real benefit.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hot-bread","title":"Fresh Bread","paragraphs":["The smell of hot, fresh bread spread throughout the street.","The old man approached the baker. He needed to buy hot bread for his grandson. The child had been asking for fresh bread for days.","People were standing in line to buy bread. He took his place in line.","There weren't many people, and his turn came quickly.","He took the change from his pocket. If he had had enough, he would have bought two loaves. But when he held out the coins, the baker waved them away and handed him a bag.","“Take these, uncle, and enjoy them.”","There were three loaves of bread in the package.","“But I only asked for one loaf.”","“Take all three. You can save some for later.”","The grandfather slowly approached the baker and whispered in his ear:","“I only have enough money for one,” he whispered.","The baker smiled:","“Please take them, uncle. It’s Friday, and every Friday we give away three fresh loaves. Enjoy them.”","“May Allah be pleased with you, my son.”","“Amen.”","He had just left the bakery when someone called out to the grandfather:","“Uncle, wait!”","The grandfather turned and looked:","“Yes, my son?”","“This is my mother’s zakat. We would be grateful if you accepted it.”","The young man put an envelope in the old man's pocket.","“May Allah accept her charity and bless her.”","As he passed the market, a woman called to him:","“Uncle, please wait a moment.”","The old man stopped and looked in the direction from which the voice came. A young woman handed him two full bags:","“We’re sharing food this Ramadan, uncle. Please accept these bags. We’d be so glad if you took them.”","“May Allah accept your kindness, my daughter.”","“Amen.”","The old man reached home with difficulty, exhausted.","“Welcome home, Father.”","“Where’s your mother?”","“In the kitchen. She’s making kaygana, the egg dish Orkhan loves.”","“Take these bags to her, please. My back is aching.”","“Grandpa, did you get fresh bread?” his grandson asked.","“Yes, plenty.”","“Hooray for Grandpa!”","“But Dad, this morning you didn’t even have money for tea.”","“Allah provided, my daughter.”","His wife entered the room:","“Where did all this come from?”","“It’s a gift for us,” he said.","The man told what had happened one by one.","When the call to prayer sounded, they broke their fast with kaygana and fresh bread. The bags also held butter and cheese, which they enjoyed with tea.","“Thank Allah for this meal. May He accept their kindness. The halva is delicious too.”","After eating, his grandson stood up.","“I’m going to my room to pray again.”","“Isn’t it early? You can pray before bed,” his grandfather said.","“I prayed at this time yesterday for warm bread and cheese, and look what came! Now I’ll ask Allah to send money for your teeth. It hurts you to chew.”","His mother smiled:","“I wish your father were here to see this.”","“He visits me in my dreams every night. He told me to pray.”","The old man suddenly remembered his jacket. He went to the hanger, put his hand in his pocket and took out the envelope. There was a large amount of money in the envelope.","His eyes filled with tears.","“What is it? Why are you crying?”","“The Lord has sent everything our grandson wished for. Look—there’s enough here for both of us to have our teeth fixed before the holiday. Praise be to Allah!”","Moral: A child’s sincere prayer and a few generous hearts can transform a family’s day. Where people are willing to share, no one need go hungry.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"credit-ledger","title":"The Credit Ledger","paragraphs":["A plainly dressed stranger entered a neighborhood shop. After looking around, he asked the shopkeeper:","“Brother, would you let me have some bread, tea, or eggs on credit?”","“There’s a man who has been taking bread on credit for three months,” the shopkeeper replied. “To be honest, I doubt he can pay. But he’s caring for three orphans. I can’t let those children go hungry. Allah is great; He will provide for us too. Take the bread you need. You don’t have to pay.” A smile spread across the stranger’s face. “I’ve found what I was looking for,” he thought.","“How much do people owe you altogether?” he asked, pointing to the credit ledger.","Surprised and a little irritated, the shopkeeper asked:","“Why do you want to know?”","The stranger answered calmly:","“Please don’t be upset, brother. I haven’t come to trouble you or waste your time. I want to buy that ledger. Would you add up all the debts and tell me the total?”","The shopkeeper looked from the stranger to the well-filled ledger. Seeing that the man was serious, he added up the debts and named the sum. The stranger took a bundle of banknotes from his jacket pocket and laid the money on the counter.","“May Allah be pleased with you, brother. You trust your neighbors and, for His sake, refuse to let orphans go hungry. You are a good man. I’ve paid everyone’s debts. Let this be my Ramadan gift to the neighborhood. Give me the ledger now. I’ll take it away and bury it, trusting that this record will speak for me on the Day of Judgment.” He took the book and left.","The shopkeeper counted the money and put it in the till, still stunned by what had happened. He looked out of the little window, but the stranger was gone. It was nearly time for iftar, the meal that breaks the daily fast, and everyone was hurrying home. Just then, a seven-year-old girl appeared in the doorway. She was the daughter of Suraiya, whose husband had died of tuberculosis.","“Uncle Ahmed,” she said shyly, “Mama asks for a loaf of bread and three eggs for iftar. We’ll pay you next month, when my uncle comes.” Her words brought the shopkeeper out of his daze. Out of habit, he reached beneath the counter for the ledger. Then he paused and smiled.","“Come, my child. Take the bread, the eggs, and some butter too. Tell your mother that uncle has already been here.”","Moral: Kindness given quietly has a special worth. Relieving someone’s debt and keeping an orphan from hunger reveal a truly generous heart.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mercedes","title":"Mercedes","paragraphs":["Mercedes was not its founder’s surname. The car took its name from the daughter of a man who loved automobiles.","Her father believed her name would bring him good fortune.","And so it happened...","The car he named Mercedes went on to win race after race.","Thanks to these successes, countless Mercedes cars have been produced since 1902.","A father’s affection helped turn his daughter’s name into one recognized around the world.","Mrs. Mercedes, who had two unsuccessful marriages, lived in Vienna. In 1929, at the age of thirty-nine, she died of cancer and was buried in Vienna.","Moral: A name given with love, a work done with love can change the world. A father's love immortalized a name.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"pay-rent-for-the-water","title":"Rent for the Water","paragraphs":["A man bought a well from a villager. But when he tried to draw water, the seller stopped him. “I sold you the well, not the water.” Unable to settle the dispute, they went before a judge.","“If the water is yours,” the judge told the seller, “you cannot store it in another man’s well. Take it away or pay him rent.”","Moral: Dishonesty can trap the person who practices it. Fair judgment considers both sides of a claim.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"scarf-seller","title":"The Scarf Seller","paragraphs":["The city was bitterly cold that night. I picked my way over the icy pavement toward the bus, relieved to reach it without falling. A boy approached, offering scarves for sale. All I wanted was to get inside and warm up.","“Would you buy one, sister?”","“No, thank you.”","“Please take one. You’ll need it.”","“I haven’t any money,” I said, hoping he would leave me alone.","“Then take it as a gift.”","“No, I already have a scarf.” I hurried onto the bus, thinking only of myself.","Before sitting down, I put my hand into my coat pocket to take out my phone. My hand touched a package. I took it out and looked. I was very surprised to see a package with a scarf inside. So the child had slipped the package into my pocket without my noticing.","I hurried back outside. The boy was still offering scarves to passengers. He was twelve or thirteen, in a thin coat with no gloves. His nose was red, but his dark eyes shone, and he greeted me with an innocent smile. I touched his shoulder and held up the package. “What’s this?”","“A gift, sister. You might need it.”","“But how will you earn anything? You’re out here working in this cold.”","“It’s all right,” he said warmly.","I held out the money.","“No, please. It’s my gift.”","If he had asked where the money had come from when I claimed to have none, I would have wanted to disappear with shame. But he asked nothing. He seemed to trust everyone as naturally as he deserved to be trusted himself.","“Please take it,” I insisted. He accepted, then began searching his pocket for change.","“Keep the change.”","“It’s too much!” In his hurry to count the coins, he dropped some.","“Then let it be my gift to you,” I said, smiling.","His smile could have melted the ice. “Thank you, sister. May Allah be pleased with you.”","“And thank you,” I thought as I returned to my seat. “You have so little, yet such a generous heart.” Outside, the cold still gripped the city, where children like him worked to help their families and somehow kept their trust in people.","The bus pulled away, leaving the boy behind in the dark. I thought of everyone out in that cold with nowhere to go. My throat tightened, and I struggled to hold back tears.","Our bus continued on its way, winding through the snow-covered mountains.","Moral: Sometimes the most helpless person shows the greatest generosity. The pure heart of a child turns material poverty into wealth.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"weight-of-the-oil","title":"The Weight of Butter","paragraphs":["A baker regularly bought a certain amount of butter from a peasant. One day he noticed that the weight of the butter he received was decreasing day by day. He accused the peasant of cheating him and complained to the judge.","“I have no scales,” the farmer explained. “I hold the butter in one hand and the baker’s one-kilogram loaf in the other. I give him the same weight of butter as he gives me in bread.”","Moral: As you treat others, you often receive the same measure in return. The bread you give comes back to you as butter.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"we-are-rich","title":"We Are Rich","paragraphs":["Near the end of the fourth century, Emperor Nintoku of Japan climbed a high tower one day and surveyed his country. Seeing not even a wisp of smoke rising, he realized his people were in poverty and that no one could cook at home.","He suspended taxes for three years and ordered that people work for their own households. He even sent the palace workers home. By the end of that time, the people were prospering.","Three years later Nintoku climbed the tower again, saw smoke rising on every side, and was sure that hearths were burning across the land. Joyfully he said to the lady beside him:","“Now we are rich!”","But the empress pointed to the palace, neglected for three years — roof leaking, flowers withered — and asked:","“You call this wealth?”","Nintoku’s answer has been remembered by the Japanese for centuries:","“When our people are poor, we are poor. When they prosper, so do we.”","Moral: True wealth is not in the treasury, but in the people’s well-being. A leader’s greatness lies not in the post, but in the meaning given to that post.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"why-am-i-poor","title":"Why Am I Poor?","paragraphs":["Once a poor man met Buddha and asked him:","“Buddha, why am I poor?”","“Because you share none of your wealth.”","“But I have nothing to give.”","“You have five kinds of wealth that you could share.”","“What are they?”","The Buddha explained:","“You can offer a smile.","“You can look at someone with compassion.","“You can speak a kind word.","“You can wish someone happiness.","“And you can lend a helping hand.”","Moral: There is always something to give: a smile, a kind word, a compassionate look, a helping hand. A person is as rich as they share.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Justice and society","slug":"edalet-ve-cemiyyet","blurb":"Right, community, and shared responsibility.","count":9,"stories":[{"stem":"bell-to-hang-on-the-cats-neck","title":"Belling the Cat","paragraphs":["A group of mice gathered to discuss how to protect themselves from a cat that had been terrorizing them.","They put forward many ideas, but could agree on none. At last, a young mouse suggested fastening a bell around the cat’s neck.","Whenever the cat approached, they would hear the bell and have time to escape. The mice greeted the plan with enthusiastic applause.","Then an old mouse, who had been listening quietly in a corner, spoke up:","“That is a clever idea, and I’m sure it would work. But there is one thing I’d like to know: who is going to put the bell on the cat?”","Moral: Making a plan is easier than carrying it out. Even the cleverest idea is of little use unless someone can put it into practice.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"woman-whose-house-was-robbed","title":"The Woman Whose House Was Robbed","paragraphs":["Long ago, an elderly woman in a certain city had her home robbed. Distraught, she went to the king to seek justice.","After hearing her story, the king asked angrily:","“How could you sleep so soundly that you didn’t notice a thief?”","The woman replied:","“We thought you were awake and watching over us, Your Majesty. That is why we felt safe enough to sleep.”","Moral: People’s sense of security grows from trust in those responsible for protecting them. When people know their ruler is keeping watch, they can sleep peacefully in their own homes.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"homeland-and-land","title":"Land and Homeland","paragraphs":["Mix earth with water and it becomes mud. Mix it with blood and it becomes a homeland.","Moral: Sacrifice gives a homeland its profound meaning. It is more than a place; it is land held sacred by those who have suffered for it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"sharing-justice","title":"Sharing Fairly","paragraphs":["Let no one weep alone","while others laugh without a care.","Let wealth cease to be an affliction,","or let the wealthy learn to share.","Let no one merely speak of love","without showing what that love means.","Let us not call a day a feast","while others have no meat to eat.","Moral: Humanity is created when joy, bread, and love are shared. If one side laughs and the other side cries, there is no justice there.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"just-verdict-of-the-frankfurt-judge","title":"The Judge of Frankfurt","paragraphs":["According to a story set in Frankfurt in 1506, a merchant lost a purse containing 800 guilders. A carpenter found it. Deeply religious, he kept it safe, certain that whoever had lost such a sum would come looking for it.","One day, after a church service, a monk announced that a visiting merchant had lost 800 guilders and offered a reward of 100 for their return.","The carpenter brought the purse to the monk.","When the merchant collected it, however, he offered only five guilders. The carpenter asked him to honor his promise. To avoid paying, the merchant claimed the purse had contained 900 guilders and accused the carpenter of taking 100. The monk defended the carpenter’s honesty, but the argument grew heated, so he took them both to court.","The judge asked the merchant to swear on the Bible that he had lost 900 guilders. He did so without hesitation. The carpenter then swore that the purse he found had contained only 800.","The judge announced his decision:","“The carpenter found 800 guilders. The merchant lost 900. Clearly, this cannot be the merchant’s purse. Since its owner has not been found, the carpenter may keep the 800 guilders. The merchant is free to continue searching for his 900.”","Moral: Greed traps a person. The stingy person who denies the truth eventually becomes a victim of his own lies; justice is on the side of the righteous.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"skins-of-the-lambs","title":"The Skins of the Lambs","paragraphs":["“It is better to shear the wool of rams than to skin lambs.”","This saying is associated with Azmzadeh Asad Pasha, who was the governor of Damascus province during the Ottoman period. The story goes like this:","One day, faced with an empty treasury, Asad Pasha asked his advisers what to do. They proposed an extra tax on the weavers of Damascus. “How much would it raise?” he asked.","“Fifty or sixty bags of gold.”","Asad Pasha objected: “These people are already struggling to make ends meet. How will they pay this tax?” The advisors said: “They will sell the gold and jewels in their houses, Pasha,” and tried to persuade him.","“I can raise it another way,” the pasha replied.","His advisers fell silent.","The next evening, he summoned the mufti to a private meeting.","“Mufti Effendi, I hear you have been secretly drinking at home, in defiance of religious law. I must report this to Istanbul, but thought I should tell you first.”","The mufti pleaded with him and offered a thousand mecidiyes to keep the matter quiet. The pasha refused. After further bargaining, they settled on six thousand.","Next he called in the judge. “Reliable sources tell me you accept bribes and use your office for personal gain.”","The judge begged not to be dismissed and disgraced. He too agreed to pay six thousand mecidiyes.","Then Asad Pasha invites the treasurer, the chief of security, the merchants and the wealthy to a meeting one by one.","By the end, he had collected two hundred bags of gold. He called his advisers together. “Have you heard of any new tax on the people of Damascus?” They had not.","“Yet I have raised two hundred bags, not fifty.”","“How did you do it?”","The Pasha replies: “It is better to shear the wool of rams than to skin lambs.”","Moral: It is more just to take the unjust profit of the wealthy than to rob the poor. The burden should be placed on the strong, not the poor.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"bedouin-whose-camel-was-stolen","title":"The Bedouin and the Stolen Camel","paragraphs":["A Bedouin riding through the desert came across a man who could barely stand, his lips parched with thirst. The man begged for water. The Bedouin dismounted and gave him a drink. As soon as he had finished, the stranger shoved the Bedouin aside, climbed onto the camel, and rode away.","Realizing that he could not recover his camel, the Bedouin shouted after the thief:","“Take the camel! It’s yours. But never tell anyone what happened here.”","Puzzled by this request, the thief stopped, looked back, and asked why.","“If you tell anyone, the story will spread,” the Bedouin replied. “Then people may stop helping strangers in need.”","Moral: Sometimes a person bears a loss quietly to protect others’ faith in human kindness. One story of betrayal can discourage countless acts of generosity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"with-this-nation-the-world-can-be-conquered","title":"The Strength of a Generous People","paragraphs":["A king went into the city in disguise to see how his people lived. He walked through the markets, observing the goods, prices, and shopkeepers.","The king entered the first shop at the beginning of the street. He greeted and said:","“Half a batman of butter, half a batman of honey, and some cheese, please.” The batman was a traditional measure of weight.","The shopkeeper greeted him warmly and weighed out the butter.","“I can sell you the honey and cheese too,” he said, “but this is my first sale today. My neighbor has not yet had one. Would you buy the rest from him?”","Pleased, the king paid and crossed the street. There he asked for the honey and cheese. The second shopkeeper weighed the honey, then said:","“Thank Allah, I’ve made my first sale. Would you buy your cheese next door? That shopkeeper is still waiting for his.”","The king, who was very impressed by what he saw, went to the shop next door and made his purchase, and said:","“With people of such character, one could conquer the world,” he said, leaving the market delighted.","Moral: A people’s strength rests in honesty and generosity. Winning hearts is a greater victory than winning territory.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"why-injustice-when-there-is-justice","title":"Why Choose Injustice?","paragraphs":["Haji Bektaş Veli:","Why hatred when there is love?","Why war when there is peace?","Why quarrel when there is brotherhood?","Why enmity when there is friendship?","Why intolerance when there is tolerance?","Why captivity when there is freedom?","Why injustice when there is justice?","Moral: When love, peace, friendship, and justice are possible, we are responsible for choosing them.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Aging and time","slug":"yaslanma-ve-zaman","blurb":"The seasons of life and what time changes.","count":3,"stories":[{"stem":"aging","title":"Aging","paragraphs":["Aging is like climbing a mountain: the higher you rise, the more tired you become and the shorter your breath, yet the wider your view of the world grows.","Moral: Growing older brings more than a loss of strength. Experience and wisdom can also give us a broader view of life.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"for-those-who-have-reached-sixty","title":"Life After Sixty","paragraphs":["It is time to use the money you have saved up so far. Use it and enjoy it. Don’t save it for those who know nothing of the sacrifices you made to earn it. This is the ideal time to enjoy peace.","Do not worry about the financial situation of your children and grandchildren, and do not be upset that you have spent your money on yourself. You have taken care of them for years, taught them everything you could. You have given them education, food, shelter and support. Now it is their duty to earn their own money. Let them find their own way.","Take care of your health without overexerting yourself. Exercise moderately, walk each day, eat well, and get enough sleep.","At this age, it is easy to get sick and difficult to stay healthy. Therefore, you should keep yourself in good shape, pay attention to your medical and physical needs. Contact your doctor; get check-ups even if you feel fine.","Do not let small troubles consume you. You have already faced much in life and carry both happy and painful memories. Do not let the past hold you back or the future frighten you. Enjoy the present; many everyday worries will soon be forgotten.","Whatever your age, keep loving your partner, your family, and life itself. A lively mind and a compassionate heart can keep you young in spirit.","Keep caring for your appearance and well-being. Visit the hairdresser, care for your nails and skin, and look after your teeth. Feeling well groomed can lift your spirits and strengthen your confidence.","Don't lose sight of age-appropriate fashion trends, but maintain your own style. Read books, flip through newspapers, watch the news. Go online, find out what people think. Make sure you have an active email account and use at least a few social networks.","Respect younger people and their opinions. They may disagree with you, but they must find their own way. Offer advice rather than criticism, and share the wisdom of your experience.","Never use the phrase \"in our time...\". Now is your time. Be a part of this time as long as you live.","Spend less time with people who leave you feeling discouraged and more with those whose company brings you joy.","Do not give up your hobbies. You can travel, walk, cook, read, dance. If you want, you can keep a cat or a dog.","Get out of the house, meet people you have not seen for a long time, and either experience something new or relive something from the past. The main thing is to get out of the house from time to time. Go to museums, walk in the park.","Try to always be polite, do not complain and criticize unless necessary. If someone has broken your heart, forgive them. Apologize to those whose hearts you have broken.","Don't carry your pain with you. It will only hurt you. It doesn't matter who is right. They say that holding a grudge is like poisoning yourself and waiting for someone else to die. Never accept that poison. Forgive, forget and move on with your life.","Laugh, enjoy yourself, and welcome the peace this stage of life can offer.","Moral: After years of caring for others, allow yourself to enjoy life too. Your happiness can brighten the lives of those around you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"what-changed-after-sixty","title":"What Changed After Sixty","paragraphs":["I asked a friend nearing seventy what age had changed in him. He replied:","“I no longer tell older people, ‘You’ve already told me that story.’ Telling it again lets them revisit a happy memory.","“I no longer feel I must correct every mistake. It is not my job to make everyone perfect. Being there for someone matters more.","“I give more compliments. They brighten my day as well as the other person’s.","“I worry less about a crease or a stain on my clothes. My worth does not lie in my appearance.","“I have learned to step away from relationships in which we cannot understand or treat each other well.","“I am no longer ashamed of my feelings. They are part of being human.","“I would rather set pride aside than destroy a relationship. Pride can leave you alone.","“I make time for things that bring me joy. My happiness is partly my responsibility.","“I have learned to ask before helping. Someone may need support without wanting the kind I offer.","“I try not to spend my days complaining about health, neighbors, relatives, or retirement. Despite the difficulties, I am alive and still have people I love.","“I have stopped saying, ‘When I was your age,’ or ‘I gave you my best years.’ Each generation faces its own circumstances.","“I pay more attention to my wife. I remember the lively young woman she was and recognize how much she needs my care now.","“I no longer blame myself for everything. I have done what I could for myself and my children.","“And I ask the Lord for strength to change what I can, patience to accept what I cannot, and wisdom to know the difference.”","Moral: Age teaches a person the greatest wisdom: peace is better than perfection, and understanding is better than being right.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"Parables and fables","slug":"temsiller-ve-meseller","blurb":"Lessons told through animals and images.","count":8,"stories":[{"stem":"fly-in-the-china-shop","title":"A Fly in a China Shop","paragraphs":["A fly could never wreck a china shop on its own. But if it flew into a bull’s ear, the panicked animal could destroy the whole place.","The fly represents superstition, falsehood, and prejudice. The bull represents an ignorant person who acts without thinking.","Moral: A small lie whispered into an ignorant ear can cause enormous destruction. Knowledge, critical thinking, and strong moral values are our best protection against it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"elephant-and-the-rope","title":"The Elephant and the Rope","paragraphs":["A traveler passing an elephant enclosure was surprised to see the enormous animals tethered by one front leg with only a short rope. There were no chains or cages. They could surely snap the ropes, yet none tried to escape. He asked a nearby trainer why they stayed so obediently.","The trainer explains:","“When they’re young, a rope like that is strong enough to hold them,” the trainer explained. “As they grow, they keep believing they can’t break it. They never realize they now have the strength to free themselves.”","The traveler was astonished. The elephants could break free at any moment, yet stayed where they were because they believed freedom was impossible.","Moral: Often we are held back by beliefs formed through past failures. To become free, we must also overcome the fear of what others might say.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"donkeys-law","title":"The Donkey’s Law","paragraphs":["One day, a donkey became king of the forest. Soon afterward, he decreed that everyone must go to bed at six in the evening and rise at six in the morning. For those twelve hours, the forest must be completely silent. Anyone who disobeyed would be punished.","For a time, the forest was quiet.","Then one morning, a rooster began crowing at five. The forest erupted in uproar. A court was hastily convened, and the donkey sentenced the rooster to death. After that, no one dared challenge the law.","Years passed, and the donkey grew old and weak. As he lay in a quiet corner of the forest awaiting death, a lion came to visit him. After asking how he was, the lion said:","“You have little time left, and there is something we want to know. What was the wisdom behind your law about sleeping from six until six? We often thought of rebelling against it, but held back for fear of disturbing the peace. We assumed there must be some wisdom in it that we failed to understand.”","The donkey smiled and replied:","“I’ve wondered about that myself for years. Why did all the wild animals follow like sheep a law born of nothing more than a donkey’s foolishness?”","Moral: A senseless law survives not only through a ruler’s power, but through the silence of those who obey it. An order is not necessarily wise. Do not be afraid to question it; nonsense left unchallenged can become an accepted rule.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"hunters-description","title":"The Hunter's Tale","paragraphs":["Until the lion learns to write, every storyteller will write the hunter's tale.","African Proverb","Moral: The storyteller decides whose courage is remembered. If you cannot tell your own story, others will tell it for you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"lions-footprint","title":"The Lion's Track","paragraphs":["A boastful but cowardly hunter entered a forest while tracking a lion. He met a woodcutter and asked:","“Have you seen a lion or any lion tracks nearby?”","The woodcutter replied:","“I’ve seen the lion itself. Come with me, and I’ll show you where it is.”","The hunter turned pale.","“No, thank you. It’s only the tracks I’m looking for, not the lion.”","Moral: A person is known not by his pretensions, but by his behavior in the moment of trial. Many people \"look for a lion\" from afar, but when they come face to face, most of them retreat.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"turtles-wrong-calculation","title":"What the Tortoise Forgot","paragraphs":["Someone asked a tortoise:","“How long will it take you to reach the next village?”","“It’s a three-day journey, allowing for rain, mud, wind, and hills. I’ll give myself six.”","Six days later, the tortoise was still missing. They searched and found it halfway there. “Why haven’t you arrived?”","“I allowed for the weather and the road,” the tortoise replied sadly. “But I forgot to allow for unkind people. As soon as I set off, someone picked me up and turned me upside down.”","Moral: You can calculate every difficulty in life, but not people with bad intentions. May Allah not let you face those who prevent you from moving forward, but those who support you.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"two-donkeys","title":"The Two Donkeys","paragraphs":["Two donkeys saw a monkey in a tree and decided to tease him. “Let’s ask whether he has a friend. If he says yes, we’ll ask what fool would be his friend. If he says no, we’ll say no fool would want to.” They approached and called, “Monkey, do you have a friend?” The monkey looked down at them.","“All I can see are donkeys. Who would you suggest?”","Moral: An attempt to make someone else look foolish can expose your own foolishness instead.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"ant-that-carries-water","title":"The Ant Carrying Water","paragraphs":["According to legend, Nimrod, the biblical ruler of Shinar, wanted to punish the prophet Abraham for standing in the way of his cruelty. He ordered a huge fire to be built in the town square, intending to throw Abraham into it. Every creature that saw the flames fled.","Meanwhile, an ant hurried toward the fire, carrying a drop of water in its mouth.","Another ant saw it and asked:","“Where are you going in such a hurry?”","“Haven’t you heard? Nimrod is going to throw the prophet Abraham into the fire!” the first ant replied anxiously.","“You can’t have seen the size of that fire,” the other ant scoffed. “There’s nothing to do but run. Do you really think you can put it out with a single drop of water?”","The first ant replied:","“Even if I can’t put it out, at least everyone will know whose side I’m on.”","Moral: Even when you cannot change the outcome, you can stand up for what is right. Being there for someone you love in a time of trouble matters in itself.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]},{"title":"History and notable figures","slug":"tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler","blurb":"Lessons from history and well-known names.","count":5,"stories":[{"stem":"magnificent-lesson","title":"A Magnificent Lesson","paragraphs":["As a child, Mehmed the Conqueror, son of the Ottoman Sultan Murad II, would not sit quietly in class and was constantly disruptive. Whenever his teacher, Akşemseddin, corrected him, the boy replied:","“You cannot tell me what to do. I am the sultan’s son!”","Troubled by the boy’s behavior, the teacher hesitated to approach the sultan. At last, he found the courage to explain what was happening.","The sultan thought for a moment and whispered a plan into the teacher’s ear. Akşemseddin protested that it would be impossible, but Murad II insisted:","“It will be done.”","The next day, young Mehmed again misbehaved and answered his teacher with the same threat. At that moment, the sultan opened the door and entered the classroom.","Following the plan, Akşemseddin pretended to be furious. He reprimanded the sultan for entering without permission, even slapped him, and ordered him to leave immediately.","Murad II lowered his head, apologized, and left. Seeing that even his powerful father had to obey the teacher’s rules, the prince was stunned.","A short while later, someone knocked. This time the sultan asked permission to enter and apologized again.","The plan worked. From that day forward, Mehmed no longer disrupted his lessons. For the sake of his son’s upbringing, Murad II had set aside even a ruler’s pride.","Moral: In education, personal example is stronger than any lecture. A responsible parent may even set aside personal pride to help a child find the right path.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"albrecht-durer","title":"Albrecht Dürer","paragraphs":["In a small village near Nuremberg lived a family with ten children. Their father worked eighteen hours a day to support the household. Despite their hardship, the two eldest sons, Albrecht and Albert, dreamed of becoming renowned artists. They knew their father could not afford to educate them both at once.","After long discussions by candlelight, the brothers decided to draw lots. The winner would study at the academy in Nuremberg, while the other worked in a nearby mine to support him. After four years, they would exchange places.","Albrecht won the draw. He left for the academy, and Albert began working in the mine.","For four years, one brother studied art while the other labored underground. Albrecht’s work soon attracted wide attention; some said that it surpassed the work of many of his teachers.","By the time he completed his studies, Albrecht was earning a good income. He returned home joyfully and told his brother:","“Dear brother, now it is your turn. Go and pursue your dream; this time I will support you.”","Albert held out his hands and answered softly:","“No, brother. It is too late. Four years in the mine have damaged my hands and every one of my fingers. I can no longer draw.”","Albrecht took his brother’s hands in his own and wept.","Centuries later, the works of Albrecht Dürer (1471–1528) continue to be displayed in leading galleries around the world. The famous drawing associated with his brother’s sacrifice is known as Praying Hands.","Moral: Behind success there often stands an unseen sacrifice. To recognize and honor that sacrifice is one of the finest expressions of humanity.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"mihrimah-sultan-and-architect-sinan","title":"Mihrimah Sultan and Architect Sinan","paragraphs":["When Mihrimah Sultan, the daughter of Suleiman the Magnificent, reached the age of seventeen, it is said that two men wished to marry her. One was Rüstem Pasha, the beylerbeyi of Diyarbakır; the other was Architect Sinan, the great master of Ottoman architecture. The name “Mihrimah” is of Persian origin: “mihr” means sun, and “mah” means moon.","Sultan Suleiman married his daughter to Rüstem Pasha. Architect Sinan, already married and approaching fifty, did not see his wish fulfilled. According to the legend, he nevertheless continued to love Mihrimah Sultan deeply.","Unable to share his life with Mihrimah, Sinan is said to have expressed his enduring love through his art.","At the palace’s commission, Architect Sinan built the Mihrimah Sultan Mosque in Üsküdar and turned it into a magnificent architectural monument. Legend says the mosque’s graceful silhouette recalls the lines of a woman whose skirts fall soft to the ground.","Later Sinan raised a second mosque for Mihrimah Sultan at Edirnekapı, one of the highest points in Istanbul. Though relatively compact in scale, it stands out for its exquisite lightness and brightness. Its slender minaret, the many windows ringing the dome, and its refined interior give the mosque a special airiness. Legend holds that some of the patterns and ornaments were worked to evoke Mihrimah Sultan’s long hair.","Thus two magnificent works of architecture said to be dedicated to one love arose in Istanbul.","The legend’s most moving moment is the meeting of the Sun and the Moon. It is said there is a spot in Istanbul from which both the Edirnekapı and the Üsküdar Mihrimah Sultan mosques can be seen. At the spring equinox, from that point, a crimson sun sets behind the minaret of the Edirnekapı mosque while, at the same moment, the moon rises behind the mosque in Üsküdar.","Mihrimah — Sun and Moon…","The sight inspires wonder. Was it the result of Sinan’s precise calculations, or did a romantic explanation grow around it later?","Whatever the truth may be, this story has become one of Istanbul’s loveliest legends, joining Architect Sinan’s art with the name of Mihrimah Sultan.","Moral: Being united with the one you love is not always granted. Sometimes a person turns that love into a work that outlives the one who felt it.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"architect-sinan-dies-in-a-house-without-water","title":"Sinan Brought Water to Istanbul but Died in a House Without It","paragraphs":["During the reign of Suleiman the Magnificent, Istanbul was growing rapidly and faced a severe water shortage. The task of solving it fell to the great architect Koca Sinan (1489–1588).","One day, the sultan summoned him and asked:","“Chief architect, people pay dearly even for a packhorse’s load of water. Can Istanbul be supplied with enough water?”","Sinan requested permission to survey the surrounding country. With his assistants, he examined wells, valleys, and streams from Çekmece to Beşiktaş and calculated where dams and aqueducts could be built.","When he returned, he told the sultan:","“It can be done, but at a heavy price: sacks filled with gold would have to be placed end to end.”","The sultan replied:","“Tell me only that it is possible. If necessary, I will place the sacks not end to end, but side by side.”","Sinan began work and created a magnificent system culminating in forty public fountains across the city. Taps were fitted so that the precious water would not be wasted.","The sultan declared the public fountains the property of the people and prohibited secret pipes into private homes. In recognition of Sinan’s service, however, he verbally allowed a private line to the architect’s house.","Years passed. Suleiman and those who had witnessed Sinan’s service died. One day, the elderly architect was summoned to the palace, where the council was investigating a complaint about the private water supply to his home.","Sinan explained that the sultan himself had granted permission. When asked for a written decree, he admitted:","“At the time, I was ashamed to ask the Ruler of the World for a document. I have no written order.”","After a long debate, the council decided not to punish Sinan for his past use of the water, but ordered the private line cut off.","Near the end of his life, Sinan fell gravely ill. When those beside him tried to moisten his lips, the tap was dry. The great architect said:","“We did not serve for profit, but for Allah’s pleasure. We await our reward in the hereafter and do not grieve that this world has denied water to our home.”","Moral: Do not place excessive trust in fame, worldly privilege, or human remembrance. True service is rendered not for personal gain, but for what one knows to be right.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true},{"stem":"three-essential-things-for-a-city","title":"Three Things a City Needs","paragraphs":["Every city needs sewers, bathhouses, and libraries. Sewers cleanse the city, bathhouses cleanse the body, and libraries cleanse the soul.","Fatih Sultan Mehmet","Moral: A city must care for the inner life of its people as well as their physical surroundings.","Stories are taken from open Internet sources; illustrations were created with AI."],"hasAudio":true,"hasImage":true}]}]}
